# Highway Rob-bery: an agent-played game of Dynasty. Scenario and steering: scenario.md; deck: seed.md.
# Every say and aside is the reply of the agent playing that seat (see log/yearN/).
# The Storyteller writes the act lines and refs the moves; the engine keeps the books.
# The narrator's lines are not part of the game: they live in narration.md, never here.
# Each chapter opens with a "# ===== CHAPTER N: <title>" marker.
setup([1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8], "highway-robbery", "Highway Rob-bery",
      "Six players · twelve years · the Warlord on the throne",
      "Six players, two pieces of each realia and three collectors for every type, so somebody is always one short. "
      "The throne changes hands four times and the lesser chairs three more, every climb sending pieces back to the "
      "auction just before the Warlord's soldiers reach them. Rob raids his way to three of his four names while the "
      "Empress pays him to read the stars. At ×4 the harvests sink Paddy into debt, and in Year 12, with one work left "
      "to build, Clark calls a revolt on that debt to stop the game ending. Reina pays the debt off in the same breath, "
      "too late. Rob, with the only soldiers on the board, is acclaimed Emperor, builds the last work and ends the game, "
      "and Reina finishes in the desert with his army.",
      short="6 players · 12 years")

# ============================== SETUP ==============================
R(0, "Setup", None, "Six players: the Emperor, the Official, the Gentry, the Silk and Rice Peasants and the Warlord (rulebook Section 4 and Appendix A). No Scholar, so the Emperor appoints someone to read I Ching and count the population, at the Scholar's salary of 5 (Note 3). Each realia province holds two pieces of its own realia, and each collector needs one from each of the other three. The Warlord starts with two soldiers and may hire one more, for 20, after any march (Section 14).")
act("Six booklets: Reina Osorio draws the Emperor, Clark Grafton the Official, Richard Baron the Gentry, Silke Weber the Silk Peasant, Paddy Doyle the Rice Peasant and Robert Land the Warlord.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
deal_grain("Rich, the Gentry, sets out the Harvest Pile and deals 60 to each player. It is the first year's harvest.")
take_stock("Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy each take two pieces of their own realia out of the box. They wait beside the board until they're sold.")
act("Clark cuts out the Province 2 Irrigation System, one square by two; Rich the Province 3 Reservoir, two by three. They're set aside until built. Nobody sits in Provinces 4 or 7, so nothing is owed there.", map_hl=[2,3])
# Reina's first prompt: who reads I Ching (then the shuffle). Rob's first prompt: his two soldiers (place_army) and his tax stance.
# ===== CHAPTER 1: Neat Handwriting
place_army({"W1":"G11", "W2":"Q26"}, "Robert Land stands his two soldiers: W1 on G11, on the strip under the Wall where Clark's Province 2 and Rich's Province 3 meet; W2 on Q26, in the sea off the coast where Silke's Province 5 and Paddy's Province 6 meet. He declares he won't pay tax on his own harvest, which the Warlord may refuse.")
say("Rob", "Evening, all. One man on the Wall road between Clark and Rich, one in a boat between Silke and Paddy. I'll be fair and tell you now that the first one I ride on is Clark. As for tax, Reina, darling, you'll get my tax the day you can come and collect it.",
 "Rob: Each soldier sits on a square touching two of my targets, so nobody can be sure which way I'll ride. Telling them it's Clark first is half true. What I really want is to watch Clark and Rich spend money guarding against me while I eat Silke and Paddy on the coast.")
say("Reina", "Rob, darling, a man who rides the Wall road ought to read the stars too. I name you keeper of the I Ching, so shuffle honestly, and there may be a scholar's salary in it at year's end. Clark, don't look so pale, I haven't forgotten you.",
 "Reina: A bandit with a title and a salary he could lose is cheaper than any army I could buy, and five a year costs a lot less than a palace. If Rob gets used to taking the crown's coin, he'll think twice before riding on the crown's men.")
act("Reina appoints Rob, the Warlord, to the empty Scholar's job: he'll read an I Ching card each year from Year 2 and keep the population counter. Whether he's paid the Scholar's 5 is hers to decide at each year's salaries. Rob shuffles the twenty cards and lays them face down.", map_hl=[1,8])

# ============================== YEAR 1 ==============================
R(1, "The first year", None, "No card is drawn. The 60 each player was dealt is the first harvest, and the tax is levied on it.")
CUR_INC.update({s:60 for s in ACTIVE})
say("Rich", "Silke, Paddy, my dears, just the customary five apiece, the smallest rent in the Middle Kingdom, and the Harvest Pile pays you back many times over. Your Majesty, the gentry of the north-west are, as ever, your most humble servants. Rob, if you're riding on Clark first, I'll keep a lamp lit for him.",
 "Rich: Five from each of them is ten a year, and none of it is taxed. That goes toward the twenty grain my set needs. Meanwhile Rob has just told the whole table he'll lean on Clark, and I won't spend a single grain stopping him.")
rents(5, note="Rent: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for 5 each, and they pay.")
say("Reina", "A gentle thirty percent this year, friends, so twelve from each of you, and nobody can call that greed. The Summer Palace goes up first, since every wall and every canal starts there. Clark gets his five for collecting, and Rob, the scholar's five is yours as long as the stars come out honest and Clark stays on his feet.",
 "Reina: The Summer Palace costs twenty, and it opens up the Wall and the Province 1 canal, so the crown can start growing next year. I'm also paying Rob a little now so that leaving Clark alone feels like something he's been paid to do.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 30%: on each province's 60 that is 18, not 12, so 72 from Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy. Rob has refused his. She'll build the Summer Palace from the crown's own grain, and pay Clark 5 and Rob 5, as the I Ching reader, at the year's end.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.30, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent is eighteen a head, friends, not twelve. Her Majesty's arithmetic is generous, but the crown's ledger isn't. Eighteen from me, eighteen from Rich, Silke and Paddy, and seventy-two goes to the treasury. Rob, your refusal is now on the books in my best hand, with the date. And Rob, the man you say you'll ride on first happens to be the man who knows where everybody's grain is kept. You might think twice about that.",
 "Clark: Every coin goes in honest, so nobody gets to call the Official a thief. My book now holds the Emperor misquoting her own tax, the Gentry 'lamp-lighting' for my funeral, and the Warlord stiffing the crown, and every one of those pages is for sale. Rob will learn soon enough that a live Clark is worth more to him than a raided one.")
build("Summer Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Summer Palace", at="G26", text="Public works: Reina builds the Summer Palace on G26, the far eastern corner of Province 1. It must stand before any Wall link or the Province 1 canal.", hover="Reina pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 870 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: each seller puts up one piece from stock and sets its minimum. Clark's Customs House opens at 4, Rich's Land Deed Scroll at 3, Silke's Dragon Jar at 4, Paddy's Wooden Bowl at 2. Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once, and the lots are sold in order; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Clark", "My Customs House opens at four, friends, and every merchant on the Wall road will be glad of it. I'm bidding on all three of the rest. I keep the receipts, so a man with the books in his hands can't be outbid for long. Rich, if you're thinking of collecting the whole set, remember who tallies your Harvest Pile.",
 "Clark: I've got 42 grain and need three pieces plus 20 more, so I'm capping each lot at seven and staying alive for the throne. I'll pay a little extra only to stop Rich finishing his set, because the Gentry who's lighting lamps for my funeral can go without his jar and his bowl.")
say("Rich", "Friends, I'll be a modest bidder tonight. A gentleman doesn't fight over crockery, and every grain we throw at each other just ends up in the Pile. My Land Deed goes up at three, a fair price for good earth. Clark, dear fellow, I'll only say this: I'd hate to see you overpay for a jar.",
 "Rich: I'm bidding low and steady on all three pieces I'm missing, and I'll go a little higher only to keep Clark from getting the jar or the bowl first. If he's slower to a full set than I am, the throne is mine to aim at. At worst I spend thirty-six and keep sixteen toward the twenty I'll need.")
say("Silke", "Rent five, tax eighteen, and I'm sitting on thirty-seven, same as Paddy. So nobody at this table gets to call either of us rich tonight. Clark, Rich, I'm bidding on your Customs House and your Land Deed, and I'm bidding honestly. Paddy, keep your Bowl's price down where friends can reach it, and maybe I'll keep the Jar cheap for you too.",
 "Silke: Paddy needs the same Customs House and Deed I do, so I'll go a couple of grain past what they're worth to stop him getting there first. I'm stopping around ten, though, because three quarters of every bid ends up in Rich's pile, not my pocket.")
say("Paddy", "Well now, there's two of everything, so there's no call for any of us to be clawing at each other. I'll put a fair few grains on each piece and not a grain past fair, and my Wooden Bowl goes up at two, honest as the rice it holds. Silke, love, you and I want the same things, so let's not make Rich's Harvest Pile rich on our account.",
 "Paddy: I've thirty-seven grain and I need three pieces plus twenty in hand, so eight apiece is my ceiling and I won't go over it. If I get all three cheap I'm closer to the top table than a rice farmer has any right to be, and if Silke goes mad bidding, she can have the first one and I'll take the second next year.")
say("Rob", "Keeper of the I Ching, how lovely, Reina. I'll shuffle as honestly as I ride. No bids from me, friends: you buy the pretty things and set them out on the board, and I'll come round later and admire them up close. Clark, put the date on that too.",
 "Rob: Every jar and bowl they buy tonight is a piece I can ride onto and take, so I'm happy to let them buy the shop. I'm keeping my 60 for a third soldier.")
DC = "Take it, then. It's going in the book at full price, with your name next to it."
DR = "Take it with my blessing. I'm a patient man, and there's another one in the stock."
DS = "Take it, then. I'll remember what you paid for it."
DP = "Ah, go on, it's yours. There's another one where that came from."
lot(2, "CH", [B(3,8), B(3,None,DR), B(6,8), B(6,None,DP), B(5,9)],
 hover=TH(("", "Clark's minimum was 4. Rich and Paddy stopped at 8; Silke would have gone to 11 to keep it from Paddy."),
          ("Silke", "I've put both pieces on the far side of my province, as far from Rob's boat on Q26 as they can go.")), at="L24")
lot(3, "LD", [B(2,7), B(2,None,DC), B(6,8), B(6,None,DP), B(5,9)],
 hover="Rich's minimum was 3. Clark stopped at 7, Paddy at 8. Silke puts the Scroll on M24.", at="M24")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(6,8), B(6,None,DP), B(2,9), B(2,None,DC), B(3,10)],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke's minimum was 4. Paddy stopped at 8; Clark went to 9 only to keep it from Rich, who would have gone to 12 to keep it from Clark."),
          ("Rich", "Both pieces are tucked deep in my province, away from the Wall road and the desert, where neither of Rob's horsemen can reach them for two years.")), at="L12")
lot(6, "WB", [B(5,7), B(5,None,DS), B(2,9), B(2,None,DC), B(3,10)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. Silke stopped at 7; Clark again went to 9 against Rich. Rich puts the Bowl on M13.", at="M13")
say("Silke", "Nine and nine, Clark, Rich, and I'll take your receipts. That's two of my three, and Rich paid ten for my Jar, so a quarter of that comes back to the silk coast. Paddy, love, you stopped at eight both times. I did notice.",
 "Silke: The only piece I'm missing is a Wooden Bowl, and Rich has the one that's been sold, so Paddy's second Bowl is the one I'll be watching.")
say("Rich", "Silke, Paddy, your lovely things will sit in the quietest corner of the north-west, safe as heirlooms. Rob, you're welcome to come and admire them, but it's a long ride for a short look. And Clark, do write it down: two pieces, twenty grain, paid in full.",
 "Rich: Now I need only a Customs House, and Clark knows it, so I'll keep smiling at him.")
march({"W1":"H10", "W2":"P25"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps from the Wall strip onto H10, in Rich's Province 3, and W2 comes ashore on P25, in Silke's Province 5. Each raids an empty square: 5 from Rich and 5 from Silke.")
hire_soldier("T21", "Rob hires a third soldier, W3, for 20 to the Harvest Pile, and stands it on T21, on his desert just below Paddy's Province 6. His army is now at its limit of three. W3 moves from next year.")
say("Rob", "Rich, you said it was a long ride for a short look, so I've started early. That's five from the north-west, and five from you too, Silke, for the sea air. Clark, I haven't forgotten you, I'm just going the scenic way. And look, a third man's camped under Paddy's rice paddies. Shuffle up, everyone, Year 2 is going to be lively.",
 "Rob: W1 is now four rows from Rich's Dragon Jar and W2 three from Silke's Land Deed. The new man at T21 waits for Paddy to buy something worth taking. Saying 'Clark first' made everyone watch the wrong door.")
salaries({2:5, 8:5}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 5 as the Official and Rob 5 as the I Ching reader, as she declared.", hover="The Emperor owes the Official at least 5 a year; an appointee's pay is hers to choose.")

# ============================== YEAR 2 ==============================
R(2, "Rain and flood", "rainflood")
act("Rob, as Reina's appointed reader, turns the first I Ching card: Spring Floods. Good harvests in Provinces 1, 3 and 6 (Reina, Rich, Paddy); bad in 2, 5 and 8 (Clark, Silke, Rob). Provinces 4 and 7 are empty. The population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Rob", "Gather round, children, and hear the stars, shuffled honestly as promised. 'Spring Floods. Heaven waters the land generously. In some places, far too generously.' Good harvests in 1, 3, 6 and 7, so well done Reina, Rich and Paddy. Bad harvests in 2, 4, 5 and 8, which means Clark, Silke, and my own poor desert. The population holds at 100 million, multiple x1, so read your first row. Clark, Silke, my condolences. Hungry neighbours make for very interesting neighbours.",
 "Rob: The card hurts me too, but it hurts Clark and Silke more, and Rich and Paddy get fat just as my men reach their doors. A rich target is worth riding to.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: Reina +40, Rich +60, Paddy +60, Clark +10, Silke +10, Rob nothing.")
say("Rich", "The floods were kind to some of us and cruel to others, so the harvest goes out exactly as the stars wrote it, to the last grain. Rent stays at the humble five. Silke, my dear, I won't squeeze a coast that's already been raided. Rob, you're a terrible guest, but at least you're a punctual one.",
 "Rich: Paddy could easily pay more and Silke can hardly pay at all, so I'm keeping both at five. Silke holds the one Customs House on the board besides Clark's stock, and I'd like her to remember me kindly.")
rents(5, note="Rent: Rich asks Silke and Paddy for 5 each again, and they pay.")
say("Reina", "Clark is quite right, eighteen a head, and thank heaven one of us can count. Thirty percent again, friends, so Clark and Silke owe only three after the floods. The canal goes through my province this year, both sections, and the crown pays every grain of it. Rob, your five stands, but a scholar who raids the gentry reads a rather short almanac.",
 "Reina: Two canal sections add twenty a year to my harvest for good, and the tax stays gentle for the two provinces the floods hit. The reserve keeps about ninety-four, enough to buy Rob off if he turns toward my palace.")
taxes(.30, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Three from me, three from Silke, eighteen each from Rich and Paddy, and forty-two to the crown, all counted twice. Thank you, Your Majesty. It's nice to be right in public. Rob, that's your second refusal, and the page is filling up. Rich, you wanted it written down, so here it is: twenty grain for two pieces, five lost to a raider, sixty in from the floods, and both your heirlooms sitting in the north-west where anyone can see them.",
 "Clark: Rich already holds the Jar and the Bowl, so he's one deed short of a claim on my job or the throne, and I've just told the Warlord exactly where those two pieces sit. I'm sitting on 59 grain and buying nothing this year, so the next Land Deed and Dragon Jar are mine. (Clark whispers to Rob that Rich's Jar and Bowl stand unguarded on L12 and M13, and offers to call his tax refusal a clerical oversight if Rob takes them before Rich finds a Land Deed. Rich's last piece is in fact a Customs House: the Storyteller tells Rob so.)")
build("Grand Canal", 80, {1:80}, canal={1:2}, text="Public works: Reina digs both sections of the Grand Canal in Province 1. Each adds 10 to her harvest from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. 790 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: one piece is left in each province's stock. Clark's Customs House opens at 20, Rich's Land Deed Scroll at 20, Silke's Dragon Jar at 2, Paddy's Wooden Bowl at 2. Each bidder gives a limit on every lot at once; the lots are sold in order.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Clark", "Correction noted, Storyteller, and noted in the book as well. Rich, it turns out the piece you're short of is mine, the very last Customs House in the kingdom, and it opens at twenty. A gentleman doesn't fight over crockery, you said, so this is your chance to prove it. Paddy, you and I want the same Deed and the same Jar, and there's one of each left. Bid high if you like, but I keep the receipts on everyone.",
 "Clark: Rich is one Customs House short of coming for my seat or the throne, and I'm the man who sets its price, so he'll pay twenty or wait a year. Meanwhile I'm putting 36 at most on the last Deed, Jar and Bowl, which leaves exactly the 20 grain a full set needs.")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, a canal through your own province, how splendid; may it carry nothing but tribute. I'll be bidding on one piece only tonight, Clark's lovely Customs House, and I'll leave the crockery to those who still need it. My last Land Deed is the last one in the stock, though, so it goes up at twenty. Good earth is worth what it's worth.",
 "Rich: That Customs House completes my set, so I'll pay almost anything for it as long as I keep the twenty grain I need on hand. The last Land Deed is mine to price, and Clark can't complete a set without it, so he can pay dearly or go without.")
say("Silke", "Bad harvest, a raid, rent and tax, and I'm down to eighteen and a half. Clark, you've got fifty-six and three pieces still to buy, and I need exactly one, the last Bowl in the Kingdom. Stand aside on it and the silk coast backs you on whatever you need next, in public, starting now. Fight me on it and you'll pay every grain I have, and three quarters of that goes to Rich's Pile.",
 "Silke: That's the last Wooden Bowl. If Clark takes it my set is gone for good, so I'm putting all eighteen on it and passing on everything else. If I win I'll have nothing left, but I'll have the set.")
say("Paddy", "Right, I'll be plain with you all, since it's the last of each piece: I stopped at eight last year and I've had a whole flood season to regret it. The rice came in well, and I mean to bid like a man who'd like to be invited upstairs someday. Silke, love, my Bowl goes up at two, like I promised, so it's in reach of an honest pocket.",
 "Paddy: There's only one of each left, so if I lose one I may never finish the set. That's why I'm putting most of the flood money down tonight and letting Rich and Clark decide how dear they want to make it for themselves. I'll keep a little back for the twenty I need in hand, and next year's rice can make up the rest.")
say("Rob", "Rich, I'll confess something, because I'm a villain and not a sneak. A little bird with very neat handwriting has told me exactly where your Jar and Bowl are sitting, and he offered to lose my tax refusals in exchange. I'll be riding there anyway, Clark, so you can keep your clerical oversight. And none of you finishes your set while I'm still at the table.",
 "Rob: Clark wanted me to do his knife work in secret, and now he gets to explain himself to Rich in public. That's worth more to me than his favour. I'll also pay a little to keep that Customs House away from Rich, because Rich is the closest to a full set.")
DC = "Fine, take it. I've written the price down, and I'll remember it longer than you will."
DP = "Fair play to you, that's more than a bowl of rice is worth to me."
lot(2, "CH", [B(6,25), B(6,None,DP), B(3,26)],
 hover=TH(("", "Clark's minimum was 20. Paddy stopped at 25; Rich would have gone to 50. Rob would have paid 10 only to keep it from Rich, under the minimum."),
          ("Rich", "Now my set is whole: Jar, Bowl and Customs House, with the grain to spare, and I've put the new piece at the far end of my province from Rob's lead rider.")), at="K16")
lot(3, "LD", [B(6,20)],
 hover=TH(("", "Rich's minimum was 20. Clark's limit was 14, under it, so Paddy's was the only bid."),
          ("Paddy", "Both pieces go on Q19 and Q20, the corner where no soldier can reach them for two rounds.")), at="Q19")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(2,14), B(2,None,DC), B(6,15)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 2. Clark stopped at 14; Paddy would have gone to 20. Paddy puts the Jar on Q20.", at="Q20")
lot(6, "WB", [B(2,8), B(2,None,DC), B(5,9)],
 hover=TH(("", "Paddy's minimum was 2. Clark stopped at 8; Silke would have gone to 18."),
          ("Silke", "I've put the Bowl on L25, beside my other two pieces in the corner furthest from Rob's boat, so he's two rounds at least from any of them.")), at="L25")
act("Every province's stock is now sold. Rich holds a full set (Customs House, Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl) and 60 grain, so he may change seats with anyone, the throne included. Silke holds a full set too (Customs House, Land Deed, Wooden Bowl) but only 13 grain, 7 short of the 20 she needs. Clark holds nothing.", map_hl=[3,5])
say("Rich", "Twenty-six grain, Clark, and worth every one. Your Customs House will sit in the north-west like a guest of honour. Paddy, you bid like a gentleman, and I don't forget a gentleman. Rob, I'm touched that you'd ride so far to see my little collection, but do mind the neat handwriting. Some clerks sell their maps to anybody.",
 "Rich: Rob has just told the whole table that Clark tried to sell him my squares, so the rival is unmasked and I never had to say a word.")
say("Paddy", "Well, would you look at that, a rice farmer with a deed and a jar. I'll keep them up in the quiet top corner of the paddies, Rob, a good long walk from any boat. Rich, it was a fair price and a fair fight on the Customs House, and I won't pretend I'm not sore over that one grain.",
 "Paddy: Rich has the last Customs House now, so the set only gets finished if I take it off him the hard way or he trades it, and I'll be watching for his price.")
say("Silke", "Nine for the last Bowl, and I'll thank you for keeping your word, Paddy. That's Customs House, Land Deed and Wooden Bowl all on the silk coast, so I hold a full set. All I need now is the grain. Rob, you said nobody finishes a set while you're at the table. Come and try, it's a long walk from P25 to my back corner.",
 "Silke: With the Jar money I'm at about thirteen grain, so I need seven more and then I can climb, and Paddy's the one I want to climb over.")
march({"W1":"I11", "W2":"O24", "W3":"S20"}, "Rob marches all three: W1 to I11 in Rich's Province 3, W2 to O24 in Silke's Province 5, W3 up from the desert to S20 in Paddy's Province 6. Each raids an empty square: 5 each from Rich, Silke and Paddy.")
say("Rob", "Three men, three doors, and fifteen grain for my trouble: five from Rich, five from Silke, five from Paddy. Silke, darling, you're short seven for your seat change, and now you're short twelve. Paddy, the quiet top corner of your paddies is two steps from my man. Don't think of it as a raid. Think of it as a house call.",
 "Rob: Every soldier moved closer to a piece, and the raid on Silke keeps her too poor to change seats. Next year Silke's Land Deed, Paddy's Jar and Rich's Jar all come within reach of my men.")
salaries({2:5, 8:5}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 5 as the Official and Rob 5 as the I Ching reader.", hover="The Emperor owes the Official at least 5 a year; an appointee's pay is hers to choose.")

# ===== CHAPTER 2: Straight Up the Steps

# ============================== YEAR 3 ==============================
R(3, "A bad harvest", "dribble1")
act("Rob, as Reina's appointed reader, turns the I Ching card: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest this year. The population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Rob", "'Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame. All provinces collect a bad harvest.' Population stays at 100 million, multiple x1, so read the x1 row, everyone. Lean year for farmers. My lads don't farm, though. They collect, and a hungry province opens its door quicker.",
 "Rob: A bad harvest leaves Silke and Paddy short of grain, and men without grain can't pay to guard a Jar or a Deed. W3 is two rounds from Paddy's pieces, so his Jar is the first one I take.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: a bad year for all. Reina +10 (her two canal sections add 20 to the bad 50), Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy +10 each, Rob nothing.")
say("Rich", "A bad year, friends, and the pile pays ten to everyone all the same. I'll ask only the usual five from each of you, Silke and Paddy. I don't squeeze a lean field. Your Majesty, the granary is in good order, as always. And Clark, my dear fellow, your ten is counted out. Do count it, I'd hate for you to think anyone here sells short.",
 "Rich: Five keeps Silke friendly and solvent, and she's the one other full set at the table, so I want her grateful and not desperate. I'm sitting on seventy-five after this, which is plenty for the throne, and that rider on I11 is one step from H11, so I mean to move before he gets any closer.")
rents(5, note="Rent: Rich asks Silke and Paddy for 5 each, and they pay.")
say("Reina", "A lean year gets a light hand, friends: twenty percent, two grain a head, and I won't hear another word about rotten seed. The Winter Palace goes up this year all the same, because a dynasty that stops building in a bad year has already started falling. Clark, your five, and Rob, your five for the almanac. Do keep the stars honest, and keep your lads off my doorstep.",
 "Reina: Eight grain in tax won't buy anything, but it buys Silke's and Paddy's goodwill, and once the Winter Palace is up the coast canals are open to me next year. Rich is handing out kindness all round, and when a man who could take any chair is that polite, I count my own chair.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 20%: 2 each from Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy on their 10, 8 in all; Rob refuses his again. She'll build the Winter Palace from the crown's own grain, and pay Clark 5 and Rob 5, as the I Ching reader, at the year's end.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.20, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Twenty percent of a bad harvest. Two a head, and I'll start with myself so nobody can say the Official skips his own line. Rich, two. Silke, two. Paddy, two. That's eight for Her Majesty, counted twice. Rob, the book's open at your page, Year 3. Pay and I'll write 'paid' in my best hand. Refuse and I'll write that down too, with the date, like the last two times.",
 "Clark: In a lean year the man holding the ledger counts for more, because the one thing people want to know is who's been short. Rich didn't stop with his own count, he dared me to check his ten, and that makes me want to check it twice.")
build("Winter Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Winter Palace", at="H21", text="Public works: Reina builds the Winter Palace on H21, in Province 1. With it standing, the Grand Canal may be dug in Provinces 5 and 6.", hover="Reina pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 770 of the works are still owed.")
act("No auctions this year: every province's stock is sold. Pieces on the board may still be traded, anyone may buy a soldier from Rob, and a player holding a full set and 20 grain may change seats.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
say("Clark", "Paddy, a word in public, so it goes in the book properly. You've got a Scroll and a Jar, and every Bowl in the kingdom is already spoken for, so that Jar is just sitting in a lean field. Thirty grain for it, cash today, in a bad-harvest year. That's twice what Silke has in her purse. You've got until the ink dries.",
 "Clark: Everything's sold, so my set has to come off somebody else's board, and Paddy's the only one holding pieces he can't finish. Thirty is a lot of grain, but once the Jar's on my page, the only seat Rich can come for is mine, and he'll find I'm already counting it. (Paddy's set needs a Customs House, not a Bowl, and Silke has 11 grain: the Storyteller tells the table so.)")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, I've admired every step you've built, and this year I'd like to climb them. The Jar, the Bowl and the Customs House are on the board and the grain is in hand, so I take the throne, with the deepest respect. Reina, sixty grain is a fine start for anyone, and I'll be glad of your counsel. Clark, my dear old friend, I hope you'll keep collecting for me. Your book has never been in better hands.",
 "Rich: That's Reina's 104 on top of my 73, so I sit down with 177, and my three pieces go back to stock before that rider on I11 can get his hands on them. Now someone else's fields are under the desert, and my first act will be finding a soldier to stand in front of them. (The reserve was in fact 92 after the Winter Palace, so 165.)")
swap(3, 1, "Rich, holding a full set and 73 grain, changes seats with Reina and takes the throne. He takes the crown's whole reserve, 92, for 165 in all. Becoming Emperor returns all his pieces to their provinces' stock: the Customs House to Province 2, the Dragon Jar to Province 5, the Wooden Bowl to Province 6; they'll be auctioned next year. Reina moves to Province 3 as the Gentry, holding nothing, and draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
     hover="Nobody had bought a soldier, so none change hands. The Winter Palace is already this year's work; the new Emperor pays the year's salaries.")
say("Silke", "Nothing on the block, so I'll keep my eleven, thank you. Rich went from sixty to a hundred and sixty-five in one sentence; Reina's back at sixty; Paddy's sitting on forty-one and a half and a Jar Clark wants for thirty. Paddy, love, he wants it because there's a Customs House coming up at next year's auction, and that's the piece you're actually short. Just so it's said out loud.",
 "Silke: I'm nine grain short of a seat and there's nothing to buy, so the smart thing is to spend nothing and let everyone else do the moving. If Clark gets Paddy's Jar cheap, Paddy's off the ladder for good, and I'd love that. But I'd rather Clark paid full price for it, and now everyone knows what that Jar is worth. (Rich held 73 before the throne, not sixty: the Storyteller tells the table.)")
say("Paddy", "Thank you kindly, Clark, but the Jar stays in my paddies. Thirty in a lean year is generous, I'll grant you, but I can count the auction block as well as anyone, and Silke's quite right about why you want it. And Silke, thanks for saying it out loud. That's one kindness back, and I've written it down in my own little book.",
 "Paddy: The Customs House Rich just gave up goes on the block next year, and it's the one piece I'm missing, so every grain I've got waits for that sale. Clark offering me thirty tells me he'll bid for it too, so I'm keeping my eye on his purse and on Rob's man at S20.")
say("Reina", "Well played, Rich. I said I was watching you, and I'll admit I watched you walk straight up the steps I built. Enjoy the Summer Palace; I'm told the view from it is splendid. I'll take the granary and the north-west, and I'll run that pile as honestly as you did. Silke, Paddy, the usual five in rent, and not a grain more in a lean year. As for next year's auction, all three of those pieces are going to see me bidding.",
 "Reina: Sixty grain and a raided province, so a soldier would actually move for me now, but every grain I keep is a bid at next year's auction, where a Customs House, a Jar and a Bowl all come up at once. A whole set is coming up in one auction, and Rich sat on the throne long enough to forget that the Gentry is the seat that goes to market.")
march({"W1":"I12", "W2":"N24", "W3":"R20"}, "Rob marches all three: W1 to I12 in Clark's Province 2, W2 to N24 in Silke's Province 5, W3 to R20 in Paddy's Province 6. Each raids an empty square: 5 each from Clark, Silke and Paddy. He hires no one; his army is at its limit.")
say("Rob", "Congratulations on the chair, Rich, it suits you. That makes it my chair, warming up for me. Clark, I told you two years ago you'd be first, so here I am in your fields at last, collecting your five. Paddy, I'm one step from your Jar now, and Silke, I'm one step from your Deed. You heard Clark: there's a lean field out there, and I've come to harvest it.",
 "Rob: Three raids for fifteen grain, and all three men finish next to something worth taking. W1 also starts south toward Rich's Winter Palace, since every palace I burn costs the new Emperor twenty.")
say("Rich", "Clark, ten for you, double the minimum. A man who keeps a book that carefully should be paid like it, and I'd hate for anyone to think the new crown is mean with its clerks. Rob, you keep the almanac and your five. Read the stars kindly, and remember that a chair keeps warmer for a man who's on good terms with the person sitting in it.",
 "Rich: The extra five buys Clark's loyalty cheaply, or at least makes his disloyalty look ungrateful, and keeping Rob on the payroll gives him a reason to point his riders somewhere other than my palaces. I'm keeping the soldier money for now, because a soldier that can't move is twenty grain standing still, and Clark's province is already between me and the desert.")
salaries({2:10, 8:5}, text="Salaries: Rich, the new Emperor, pays Clark 10 as the Official and keeps Rob on as the I Ching reader at 5.", hover="The Emperor owes the Official at least 5 a year; an appointee's pay is the Emperor's to choose.")

# ============================== YEAR 4 ==============================
R(4, "The Emperor's Share", "share")
act("Rob, as the Emperor's appointed reader, turns the I Ching card: The Emperor's Share. Every province collects an average harvest, and every province with a public work splits that work's extra harvest with the Emperor. The only works stand in Province 1, the Emperor's own, so no grain changes hands. The population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Rob", "'The Emperor's Share. The Emperor would like to remind you who paid for the ditch. All provinces collect an average harvest. Every province with a Public Works splits the extra harvest it produces 50/50 with the Emperor this round.' The population holds at 100 million, multiple x1, so everyone reads the x1 row. Lovely card, Rich. The stars are kind to you, and so am I. Kind enough to keep reading them, anyway.",
 "Rob: The card fattens Rich's purse, and a fat Emperor is the one worth robbing, so the Winter Palace now costs him twenty instead of costing me anything. My other two men are one step from Paddy's Jar and Silke's Deed, and next march I start taking pieces. (The only works are the Emperor's own, so the Share pays him nothing.)")
harvest("Reina, now the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: an average year. Rich +35 (his canal sections add 20), Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy +35 each, Rob +10.")
say("Reina", "Thirty-five to every hand, Rob ten, and the pile pays it all gladly. Silke, Paddy, five each, as I promised, and not a grain more. Rich, do enjoy those canals. I dug them for whoever ended up sitting there, it seems. And Rob, a raider who visits the Official, the Silk Peasant and the Rice Peasant in one year will find the Gentry is the only friend he hasn't robbed yet. I'd keep it that way.",
 "Reina: Ninety-five grain plus ten in rent makes a hundred and five going into an auction where all three pieces of my set are up at once. I'm keeping the peasants sweet and Rob pointed at someone else, so that every grain I have goes to the block.")
rents(5, note="Rent: Reina, the new Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for 5 each, and they pay.")
say("Rich", "Friends, it was a lean year, then an average one, and the kingdom still owes seven hundred and seventy in works, so I'm asking half this once, and every grain of it goes back into the ground. The Grand Canal goes into Paddy's paddies this year, at the crown's expense, ten more a year for the south-east forever, and Clark gets his ten again. Silke, my lady, your coast is next in line. You have the Emperor's word on it.",
 "Rich: Silke holds a full set, and at any rate under fifty she'd keep twenty grain, enough to walk up my steps; at half she's left with eighteen and a half, and she can't. The canal gives the coast a reason to thank me while I squeeze it, and I've promised Silke the next canal, so she gets the next canal, to the letter. (A peasant can't take the throne in any case, but twenty would let her take another seat.)")
act("Rich declares a tax of 50%: 17.5 each from Clark, Reina, Silke and Paddy on their 35, 70 in all; Rob refuses his. He'll dig one section of the Grand Canal in Paddy's Province 6 from the crown's grain, and pay Clark 10 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1,6])
taxes(.50, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Fifty percent, Your Majesty. Bold, for a man who got the chair four minutes ago. Seventeen and a half a head, me first, then Reina, Silke and Paddy, and that's seventy on the table, counted twice. Rob, Year 4, your page: you've just helped yourself to five of my grain, so I'll leave the space for your tax blank and let the whole table see what you write in it. Paddy, keep your Jar. I'm a patient man, and the book keeps longer than a harvest.",
 "Clark: Rich has the throne and has gone to fifty percent in his first year, and every grain I hand him tonight goes into the grievance column under his new name. Better still, his Customs House is coming back to my province's stock, I'm sitting on more grain than anyone outside the palace, and next year's auction is the first one I've walked into as the richest bidder. (The returned pieces are auctioned this year, and after the tax Rob's 105 tops Clark's 98.)")
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={6:1}, text="Public works: Rich digs one section of the Grand Canal in Paddy's Province 6, at the crown's expense. It adds 10 to Paddy's harvest from next year.", hover="Rich pays the 40, into the Harvest Pile. 730 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: the three pieces Rich returned to stock come up in province order. Clark's Customs House, Silke's Dragon Jar, Paddy's Wooden Bowl. Each seller sets the minimum for their own lot; Clark, Reina, Silke, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once, and the lots are sold in order. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,5,6])
say("Clark", "My Customs House opens at fifteen, Reina, since you'll be wanting it and I like the look of a fair start. As for the Jar and the Bowl, I've lost both of them by a single grain before, and both are in the book with the date. I don't intend to lose one by a grain again.",
 "Clark: Reina needs all three of these pieces and has eighty-seven grain to cover them, so every grain she spends on my Customs House is a grain she can't put against me on the Jar and the Bowl. If I come away with both, I'm one Scroll short of a throne, and only two people hold a Scroll, one of them broke and the other already in my book.")
say("Reina", "Fifty percent and a canal in Paddy's paddies. Rich, you've learned in four minutes what took me two years: build first and explain later. I'll be bidding on all three of these, friends, and I'll say so plainly. Clark, you and I both want the Jar and the Bowl, and I'd rather not feed three quarters of our purses to the pile fighting over them. If you'd like to talk about splitting them, I'm listening, in public, before the gavel.",
 "Reina: I don't need to win everything this year. I need to stop Paddy finishing his set with that Customs House, and to make Clark pay full price for anything he takes from me. Whatever I take home now, I finish the set next year with a full granary behind me.")
say("Silke", "Eighteen and a half in my purse, friends. I did count it, and so did Rob, twice. I'm not bidding on anything. My Jar opens at six, and that's cheap for the last Jar in the kingdom. Reina, you've got eighty-seven and a half and nothing on the board, so I'd think it's worth six to you. Paddy, love, that Customs House is the one piece you're missing, and Reina's after it too. Mind the bidding doesn't send most of your forty-nine to the pile.",
 "Silke: If the Jar sells at six or more, my quarter of the price takes me from eighteen and a half to twenty, and twenty is all I need to change seats. I'd rather Paddy and Reina spend their grain fighting over the Customs House than spend any of mine.")
say("Paddy", "Reina, I'll put it plainly, because I lost the last one of these by a grain and I've not slept right since. That Customs House is the one piece I'm short, and you've got two others on the block to chase. Stand aside on it and you have my word in front of the whole table that I'll not come for the Gentry's seat. And Clark, no hard feelings about the Jar. My Bowl opens at eight, which is fair for a good piece of south-eastern pottery.",
 "Paddy: Thirty-eight's as far as I'll go. It leaves me eleven, and with an average harvest and Rich's canal that gets me back over twenty by next year's seat change. Rob's man is one step from my Jar, so if I'm going to climb it has to be soon, before he walks in and takes my set apart.")
say("Rob", "Nothing for me, friends. I've never paid for a piece in my life and I don't mean to start now. Go on, bid each other into the dirt. Whatever you buy today, I'll pick up later for nothing. Reina, Clark: when you've settled who gets the Jar and the Bowl, put them somewhere I can find them.",
 "Rob: Every grain they bid goes three-quarters into the pile, and whatever they buy goes onto the board, where my men are waiting. I'd rather let them pay for these pieces and then raid them.")
DP = "Well, that's two Customs Houses I've lost now. I'll be keeping an eye on Silke's."
DR = "Take it, with my compliments. The pile thanks you more than I do."
lot(2, "CH", [B(6,38), B(6,None,DP), B(3,39)],
 hover=TH(("", "Clark's minimum was 15. Paddy stopped at 38; Reina would have gone to 30 for her set and to 40 to keep it from Paddy."),
          ("Reina", "It cost me fourteen more than I'd hoped, but Paddy's set stays broken and I've finally got a piece on the board, tucked well behind the line. Now Clark holds the Jar and the Bowl I need, and I hold the Customs House he'll want, so next year we'll be trading.")), at="M10")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(3,25), B(3,None,DR), B(2,26)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 6. Reina stopped at 25; Clark would have gone to 40, and to 48 to keep it from Reina. Clark puts the Jar on G16.", at="G16")
lot(6, "WB", [B(3,25), B(3,None,DR), B(2,26)],
 hover=TH(("", "Paddy's minimum was 8. Reina stopped at 25; Clark would have gone to 40, and to 48 to keep it from Reina."),
          ("Clark", "Two pieces down, one Scroll to go, and only Silke and Paddy have one, with Silke holding eighteen grain and very good reasons to sell. I've put them in opposite back corners so a single soldier can't take both in one march. (After her quarter of the Jar, Silke holds 25.)")), at="J16")
say("Reina", "Thirty-nine, and twenty-nine of it straight to the pile. Paddy, I'm sorry. I'd have taken your word if a word could keep you out of my seat for long, but a Customs House keeps you out for certain. Clark, congratulations on the Jar and the Bowl. You did say you wouldn't lose them by a grain, and you were exactly as good as your word. Rob, I'm putting this one deep in the north-west, where you'll need a long walk and a good map.")
say("Clark", "Twenty-six apiece, and a grain ahead of the Empress-that-was on both. I'll call that the debt repaid, with interest. Rob, you asked where to put them. Back row of Province 2, as far from your boots as the map goes. Come and find them if you fancy the walk.")
say("Silke", "Twenty-five grain and a full set, friends, and I counted it twice, the way Clark taught us. Clark, love, you spent fifty-two today on a Jar and a Bowl to get ahead of me by a grain. I'll take the book, the seat, and your purse with it. Rich, I'll collect for you just as carefully as he did, and I'll want my ten a year as well.",
 "Silke: Clark had the biggest purse of the three I could replace, and he'd just bought two pieces that put him close to a set of his own. Now those pieces go back to stock and he's starting again from sixty. I've climbed to the upper class before Paddy, which was the plan from the start.")
swap(5, 2, "Silke, holding a full set and 25 grain, changes seats with Clark and becomes the Official. She takes Clark's 55.5, for 80.5. Her Customs House, Province 2's own piece, goes back to its stock; her Land Deed Scroll and Wooden Bowl move with her to Province 2. Clark returns the Dragon Jar and Wooden Bowl he has just bought to the stock of Provinces 5 and 6, moves to Province 5 as the Silk Peasant, holding nothing, and draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
     hover="Nobody had bought a soldier. The returned pieces are auctioned next year. Silke's new seat needs a Land Deed, a Dragon Jar and a Wooden Bowl.",
     at={"LD":"H16", "WB":"I16"})
say("Silke", "Back row of Province 2, as far from the Wall as the map goes. Clark, love, you picked the spot, I'm only borrowing it. Rob, it's a long walk from I11 and I'll be counting your steps. Now, one Dragon Jar and I'm done, and I've got eighty and a half grain to pay for it.",
 "Silke: H16 and I16 are both on the card's list of squares out of every soldier's reach this year and next, so Rob's W1 can't get at them for two rounds. Next year's auction is about one piece for me: whoever sells a Dragon Jar, I'm bidding. (Rob's W1 stands on I12, not I11.)")
march({"W1":"I13", "W2":"M24", "W3":"Q20"}, take={"W3":("DJ","T17")}, text="Rob marches: W3 steps onto Q20 in Paddy's Province 6 and takes his Dragon Jar instead of the 5, the desert's first piece of realia; Rob sets it on T17, out in the sand. W1 steps onto I13 in Province 2, Silke's now, and W2 onto M24 in Province 5, Clark's now, where Silke's Deed stood until she moved; Silke and Clark pay 5 each.")
say("Rob", "Paddy, I told you two years ago it'd be your Jar first, and here I am, a man of my word. It goes in my sand at T17, and you're welcome to visit. Silke, congratulations on the book. My lad on I13 is four steps from your Scroll and Bowl in the back row, and he's started walking. Clark, you're a peasant now, so here's your first visit, five off the new farmhouse.",
 "Rob: That's my first piece taken, and it breaks Paddy's set. W1 is now heading straight for Silke, who needs only a Jar to climb again. If I take her Bowl or Scroll in four rounds, her climb comes apart. (W1 is in fact three rounds from both.)")
say("Rich", "Silke, my lady, congratulations on the book. I declared ten for the Official and the Official gets ten, every grain, exactly as she asked. Rob, five for the almanac again. The stars were very good to the crown last year, and I'm hoping they stay sentimental. Clark, welcome to the coast, old friend. I promised its next canal to Province 5, and a promise to a province outlasts whoever's living in it.",
 "Rich: Silke is one Dragon Jar short of my chair with eighty grain in her purse, and the only Jar left goes on the block next year, so that auction is the real fight. I keep my word to the letter: I promised Province 5 a canal, so Clark gets to farm the kindness I meant for her.")
salaries({2:10, 8:5}, text="Salaries: Rich pays Silke 10 as the Official and Rob 5 as the I Ching reader.", hover="The Emperor owes the Official at least 5 a year; an appointee's pay is the Emperor's to choose.")

# ===== CHAPTER 3: A Chair So Keen to Be Sat In
# ============================== YEAR 5 ==============================
R(5, "Imperial Charity", "chest")
act("Rob, as the Emperor's appointed reader, turns the I Ching card: Imperial Charity. Every province collects an average harvest, the population holds at x1, 100 million, and the Emperor gives every other player 2 from his reserve.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Rob", "\"Imperial Charity. Moved by the suffering of his people, the Emperor opens his purse. Slightly.\" Average harvest everywhere, no disasters, population holds at a hundred million, times one, so read the x1 row. And Rich hands every one of us 2 from his reserve. Including me, Rich. I'll put yours toward a nicer torch for the Winter Palace.",
 "Rob: That card has the crown paying the Warlord. Good for my temper, and it costs Rich twelve he'll miss more than I will. W1 is three steps off Silke's Bowl, and I'm still riding that way. (Five players get 2 each, so it costs Rich 10.)")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: an average year. Rich +35 (his canal sections add 20), Paddy +45 (his canal section adds 10), Clark, Reina and Silke +35 each, Rob +10.")
act("Imperial Charity: Rich gives 2 from the reserve to each of the five other players, 10 in all.", deltas={1:-10, 2:2, 3:2, 5:2, 6:2, 8:2}, map_hl=[1])
say("Reina", "The pile pays as the card says: thirty-five a head, forty-five for Paddy and his new canal, ten for Rob. Rent's five from each of you again, Clark, Paddy, the same as last year and not a grain more. A gentle landlord remembers who sells her pottery, and I'd hope a peasant remembers who kept his rent low.",
 "Reina: Ten grain puts me at eighty-three and a half. Clark has the Jar beside the board and Paddy has the Bowl, so this year I squeeze nobody, because the two men I'm keeping happy are the two I need to buy from.")
rents(5, note="Rent: Reina asks Clark and Paddy for 5 each, and they pay.")
say("Rich", "Last year I asked for half, and I said it would be this once, so this year it's forty, and the crown is grateful for every grain of it. And Clark, I told you a promise to a province outlasts whoever's living in it. The canal comes to your coast this year, paid for by the throne, ten more for you every harvest from next year on. Silke, your ten, as always, with my thanks.",
 "Rich: Forty percent still takes fourteen out of Silke's purse before the Jar goes on the block, and the canal means Clark has to stand there and thank me in front of everyone. Kept to the letter, and it costs me less than it costs him.")
act("Rich declares a tax of 40%: 14 each from Silke, Reina and Clark on their 35, and 18 from Paddy on his 45, 60 in all; Rob refuses his. He'll dig one section of the Grand Canal in Clark's Province 5 from the crown's grain, and pay Silke 10 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1,5])
taxes(.40, collector="Silke, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Silke", "Forty percent, ladies and gentlemen, book's open. Clark, fourteen out of your seventy-three. Reina, fourteen out of sixty-nine and a half. Paddy, eighteen out of seventy-nine and a half, because that new canal pays the crown too. My own fourteen goes in first so nobody has to wonder. Sixty for the throne. Rob, I know you're refusing, but I'm writing it down anyway: a hundred and thirty-two grain in that purse and nothing in my book.",
 "Silke: I'll be paying the tax out of a hundred and eight with a Bowl and a Deed on the board, and the only Jar I need is sitting in Clark's stock. Rob's man on I13 is three rounds off, so this is the year I buy that Jar off Clark, whatever it costs me.")
act("The purses Silke names are what each holds after paying: before the tax Clark held 87, Reina 83.5 and Paddy 97.5.", map_hl=[3,5,6])
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={5:1}, text="Public works: Rich digs one section of the Grand Canal in Clark's Province 5, at the crown's expense. It adds 10 to Clark's harvest from next year.", hover="Rich pays the 40, into the Harvest Pile. The Winter Palace, which the coast canals need first, already stands. 690 of the works are still owed.")
act("The auctions: the three pieces returned to stock last year come up in province order. Silke's Customs House (Province 2), Clark's Dragon Jar (Province 5), Paddy's Wooden Bowl (Province 6). Each seller sets the minimum for their own lot; Silke, Reina, Clark, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once, and the lots are sold in order. The Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,5,6])
say("Clark", "Rich, a canal on my coast, dug and paid for. I'm writing that under 'promises kept', and believe me, that page is short. Now the Jar: twelve to open, ladies. Silke, Reina, you both need it and there's only the one, so do sort out between you how much you want it.",
 "Clark: Two of the three pieces I'm missing are for sale this year, and I'm keeping a cap on both so there's grain left for the twenty I'll need at the end. The Jar's on my own lot, so every grain Silke and Reina throw at each other drops a quarter into my purse.")
say("Paddy", "Thank you, Reina, for the five, I mean that. And Rich, thank you for the charity. Now, I've lost two Customs Houses by one grain each, so this year I'm bidding one grain past careful, and I'll tell you that straight. Clark, Silke, I'm not out to start a war over it. I'd just like to go home with something this time.",
 "Paddy: Thirty-one and thirty is all I'll go to. That leaves me about twenty in the purse if I win both, and I'd need that to sit at the top table. If Silke wants the Jar badly enough she can outspend me, but she'll pay a lot more than twelve for it.")
say("Silke", "Paddy, love, I've done the arithmetic for you: seventy-nine and a half in your purse, and that's every grain you can bid. I've got a hundred and eight and a half. Reina's sixty-nine and a half doesn't get there either. So if either of you wants to stand aside now and keep your grain for the Bowl or the works, I'll remember it. If not, Clark gets a very nice quarter and the Pile gets fat.",
 "Silke: Eighty-two beats any purse that wants this Jar and still leaves me more than the twenty I need to climb, so the set's finished this year, before Rob's man gets to my back row. My Customs House can go cheap at two; I don't need the money, I need the seat.")
say("Reina", "Silke, your arithmetic is lovely and I don't dispute a grain of it. The Jar's yours if you want it badly enough, and I won't make the Pile fat out of spite. I'll put in a polite number and no more. The Bowl, though, is the one I've come for, Paddy, and I'd sooner pay you for it than see it go to anyone else.",
 "Reina: Silke can outbid me on the Jar, so I'll put in twenty to give her a small fright and keep my grain for the Bowl, where only Clark stands in the way and he'll have spent on the Customs House first. I can find a Jar next year. A Bowl lost to Clark finishes his set, and that's a throne gone. (Clark holds nothing, and would still need a Land Deed Scroll.)")
say("Rob", "Ten, not twelve, Rich. My mistake, and you're a cheaper saint than I made you out to be. As for the auction, I still haven't paid for a piece in my life and I'm not starting with a soup bowl. Bid up, ladies. Whatever you buy goes on the board where my men can see it, and Silke, you've already got two out there three steps from my W1.",
 "Rob: Every grain they bid is grain they don't have when I come calling, and three quarters of it goes into Reina's pile. Let them buy the crockery, and I'll collect it later.")
DC = "Take it, then, and I'll write down what you paid. It's going in the book."
DR = "Take it, with my compliments. I'll remember the price."
DP = "Well, there's one grain gone again. It's somebody else's year, then."
lot(2, "CH", [B(5,24), B(5,None,DC), B(6,25)],
 hover=TH(("", "Silke's minimum was 2. Clark would have gone to 22 for his set and to 24 to keep it from Paddy; Paddy would have gone to 31."),
          ("Paddy", "I finally have a Customs House, and I've put it at S25, as far from Rob's soldiers as my land goes. Silke beat me to the Jar by one grain, which makes three times now, so the Dragon Jar is the piece I'm still hunting.")), at="S25")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(3,20), B(3,None,DR), B(6,30), B(6,None,DP), B(2,31)],
 hover=TH(("", "Clark's minimum was 12. Reina stopped at 20 and Paddy at 30; Silke would have gone to 82."),
          ("Silke", "G16 is the far corner, out of every soldier's reach this year and next, and the set is finished with plenty more than twenty in hand. Now all I have to decide is whose chair I want, and before Paddy gets his Bowl back I want it to be one he can't climb into.")), at="G16")
lot(6, "WB", [B(5,18), B(5,None,DC), B(3,19)],
 hover=TH(("", "Paddy's minimum was 6. Clark stopped at 18; Reina would have gone to 49."),
          ("Reina", "It goes on M12, next to my Customs House and out of reach of every soldier for two rounds. Two pieces down, and all I need now is a Jar, and I've got sixty grain left to hunt one down next year. (She holds 50.5.)")), at="M12")
say("Paddy", "Twenty-five! Clark, I'm sorry, truly, but I lost two of these by a grain each, and this time the grain went my way. It's going right down in the bottom corner of my paddies, Rob, so your lads will need good boots to reach it. And Reina, enjoy the Bowl. Nineteen is a fair price and I'm glad it went to a good home.")
say("Silke", "Thirty-one. Clark, that's seven and three quarters for you, and Paddy, thank you for the twenty-five for my Customs House, a quarter of which comes back to me. That's eighty-three and three quarters in my purse and a full set on my board. Rob, count the steps from G16 to your man again. I'll wait.")
act("A seller's quarter is rounded down to the half million: Clark keeps 7.5 of the 31 and Silke 6 of the 25, so Silke holds 83.5.", map_hl=[2,5])
say("Reina", "Nineteen for a Bowl, Paddy. I'd have paid more, so think of the difference as a thank-you for your manners. And Clark, I spoke out of turn about your set, my apologies; I was counting someone else's pieces. Rob, it sits well back from the Wall, so do tell your men to admire it from a distance.")
say("Silke", "Rich, I've counted it for you: two hundred and fifty-seven in the reserve, plus my eighty-three and a half, is three hundred and forty and a half. The book's yours now, darling, with sixty from the pile to start you off. I'll take the throne and the purse, and Clark, the canal promise stands, because I keep the crown's word.",
 "Silke: A full set takes the highest chair there is, and I'd rather sit on it than leave it there for Paddy to aim at. It costs me every piece on the board, but Rob's man was three steps from all of them anyway.")
swap(2, 1, "Silke, holding a full set and 83.5 grain, changes seats with Rich and takes the throne. She takes the crown's whole reserve, 257, for 340.5 in all. Becoming Empress returns all her pieces to their provinces' stock: the Land Deed Scroll to Province 3, the Dragon Jar to Province 5, the Wooden Bowl to Province 6; they'll be auctioned next year. Rich moves to Province 2 as the Official, holding nothing, and draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
     hover="Nobody had bought a soldier, so none change hands. The Province 5 canal is already this year's work; the new Empress pays the year's salaries.")
march({"W1":"J12", "W2":"L24", "W3":"Q19"}, take={"W3":("LD","O18")}, text="Rob marches: W3 steps onto Q19 in Paddy's Province 6 and takes his Land Deed Scroll instead of the 5, setting it on O18 in the desert. W1 steps onto J12 in Province 2, Rich's now, and W2 onto L24 in Clark's Province 5; Rich and Clark pay 5 each. He hires no one; his army is at its limit.")
say("Rob", "Congratulations on the throne, Silke. I've been warming it for years, so do mind the splinters. Paddy, I'm sorry, but your Scroll is coming to see the desert, and Clark, that's five for the toll of having a coast. Reina, I'm telling you now so it's fair: W1 is four steps from your Bowl and your Customs House, and \"well back from the Wall\" isn't nearly far enough.",
 "Rob: Paddy's Scroll doesn't count toward my objective, but without it he can't climb, and nobody climbs while I'm still riding. Now the palaces belong to Silke, so every one I burn costs her twenty, and W2 is on its way to the Winter Palace.")
act("After the march, W1 on J12 is 3 rounds from both of Reina's pieces, M10 and M12.", map_hl=[3])
say("Silke", "Rich, ten, because that's what you paid me and I'm not cheaper than you. Rob, you keep the cards, and five a year, which is three hundred and twenty-five and a half left in the reserve, and I'll know if it moves. Do read me something nice next year, darling.",
 "Silke: Paying Rich his ten keeps him from grumbling over the book, and keeping Rob on the payroll is cheaper than him visiting the Winter Palace when W2's four steps away. Every grain I spend now is meant to keep me on this seat.")
salaries({2:10, 8:5}, text="Salaries: Silke, the new Empress, pays Rich 10 as the Official and keeps Rob on as the I Ching reader at 5.", hover="The Emperor owes the Official at least 5 a year; an appointee's pay is the Emperor's to choose.")

# ============================== YEAR 6 ==============================
R(6, "Population Doubles", "double1")
act("Rob, as the Empress's appointed reader, turns the I Ching card: Population Doubles. Every province collects a good harvest, and the population doubles to x2, 200 million: every province eats twice as much. From this year, new land may be bought.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Rob", "\"Population Doubles. Fat years make large families.\" A good harvest for every province, and the counter goes from a hundred million to two hundred million, so read the x2 row, everyone. Something nice for you, Your Majesty, just as you asked: twice as many subjects, and twice as many mouths.",
 "Rob: A good harvest fills every purse, which leaves more on the board for my men to collect. Twice the people also means twice the hunger later, and hungry provinces don't defend their crockery.")
harvest("Reina, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: a good year at x2, when every seat eats twice as much. Rich, Reina +20; Clark and Paddy +30 (each has a canal section); Silke +0 (her two canals cover the crown's doubled table); Rob +0.")
say("Reina", "A good harvest at twice the mouths: twenty for Rich and me, thirty each for Clark and Paddy with their canals, and nothing for the throne or the desert, so congratulations on your splinters, Your Majesty. Rent stays at five, gentlemen. But you're both richer than your landlady now, and if the next card doubles us again, we'll be talking about it over tea.",
 "Reina: Ten grain takes me to eighty and a half, and with the Jar back in stock I need every grain of it. I'm keeping the peasants sweet this year, because if Silke and I fight over that Jar again, I'll need Clark and Paddy to stay out of the bidding. (Silke, as Empress, doesn't bid.)")
rents(5, note="Rent: Reina asks Clark and Paddy for 5 each, and they pay.")
say("Silke", "Forty percent, same as Rich, so nobody can say the new girl got greedy: twenty-four grain from the four of you that pay, and none from Rob, I'm sure. Clark, I'm finishing your canal, that's forty from the crown, and anyone who buys new land gets half of every square from me. Rich, ten for the book. That's three hundred and twenty-five and a half going out as fast as it comes in, so don't anyone call me stingy.",
 "Silke: With twice the mouths the throne loses money in an average year, so four squares at home stop the bleeding, and paying half of everyone else's land keeps the revolt talk quiet. Finishing Clark's canal buys me the one peasant who isn't Paddy.")
act("Silke declares a tax of 40%: 8 each from Rich and Reina on their 20, and 12 each from Clark and Paddy on their 30, 40 in all, not 24; Rob refuses his. She'll dig the second section of the Grand Canal in Clark's Province 5 from the crown's grain, pay Rich 10 at the year's end, buy four squares of new land for Province 1, and pay half of every square another province buys this year.", map_hl=[1,5])
taxes(.40, collector="Rich, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, the book is open, and I'll keep it the way you kept it for me, to the grain. It's forty, not twenty-four: eight from me, and mine goes in first, eight from Reina, twelve each from Clark and Paddy for those lovely canals. Rob, I've written down your refusal, with the greatest respect.",
 "Rich: I sat on that chair for three years and she took it in one auction step. So I'll be the most correct Official this province has ever had, and I'll collect every grain for her while I count every grain she spends.")
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={5:1}, text="Public works: Silke digs the second section of the Grand Canal in Clark's Province 5, at the crown's expense. With both sections, Clark's harvest gains 20 a year from next year.", hover="Silke pays the 40, into the Harvest Pile. 650 of the works are still owed.")
act("New land: from this year any province may buy squares of new land, 20 each, adding 20 to its harvest from next year. Silke will pay half of every square another province buys this year. Clark's Province 5 may take the sea; Paddy's Province 6 the sea or the Warlord's land; Provinces 2 and 3 only once the Wall above them stands; the crown may take the sea now.", map_hl=[1,5,6])
say("Silke", "Thank you, Rich, forty it is, and I'll take the correction: you count better than I thought. Four squares for the crown, west side, eighty to the Pile, and every one of them pays for itself by next harvest. Rob, they're on the far side from your W2, so do keep walking.",
 "Silke: Four squares turns the crown's minus twenty-five into plus fifty-five in an average year, and I put them on the western edge where Rob's man on L24 has the longest walk.")
buy_land(1, ["E23","F23","F24","F25"], {1:80}, "New land: Silke buys four squares of sea for Province 1, E23 and F23 to F25, for 80 from the crown's reserve. Each adds 20 to the crown's harvest from next year.")
say("Clark", "Four squares, Your Majesty, half from you as you announced, and I'm writing it down under 'promises kept' right beside Rich's canal. That's two of you on the page now, Silke, so mind you stay there. And Rob, they're on the far shore, so tell W2 to bring a boat.",
 "Clark: Squares at ten grain apiece that pay twenty a year are the cheapest leverage in the kingdom, and four of them cover me if the population doubles again. I put them in the far south-east corner, as far from Rob's W2 as the coast goes.")
buy_land(5, ["Q26","Q27","P27","O27"], {5:40, 1:40}, "New land: Clark buys four squares of sea for Province 5, Q26, Q27, P27 and O27, paying 40; the crown pays 40. Each adds 20 to his harvest from next year.")
say("Paddy", "Your Majesty, I'll take you at your word, half a square each. Four squares, then, down at the bottom of my paddies, forty from me and forty from the crown. Twice as many mouths in the south-east means twice as many hands, and I'd like them all planting rice and not out wandering the desert with Rob.",
 "Paddy: With Silke paying half, every square costs me ten and pays twenty a year, so that's the best bargain I've seen in this game. I'll still have about thirty grain toward next year's Deed and Jar, and I've kept the new land in the bottom corner, out of Rob's way.")
buy_land(6, ["R25","R26","S26"], {6:30, 1:30}, "New land: Paddy buys three squares of sea for Province 6, R25, R26 and S26, paying 30; the crown pays 30. Q26, the fourth he asked for, went to Clark a moment before: land is settled in table order.")
say("Paddy", "Beaten to a square by Clark, and by a moment, not even a grain this time. Fair enough, neighbour. I'll take R27 on the same terms, Your Majesty, ten from me and ten from the crown, and the south-east will feed four times the mouths if it has to.",
 "Paddy: That fourth square is the one the card says I'd need if the population doubles again, and it cost me only ten. I'll have thirty-two in the purse and a harvest of eighty-five coming toward next year's Deed and Jar.")
buy_land(6, ["R27"], {6:10, 1:10}, "New land: Paddy buys a fourth square of sea for Province 6, R27, paying 10; the crown pays 10. Each of his four adds 20 to his harvest from next year.")
act("The auctions: the three pieces Silke returned to stock come up in province order. Reina's Land Deed Scroll (Province 3), Clark's Dragon Jar (Province 5), Paddy's Wooden Bowl (Province 6). Each seller sets the minimum for their own lot; Rich, Reina, Clark, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once, and the lots are sold in order. The Empress doesn't bid.", map_hl=[3,5,6])
say("Rich", "An Official with an empty board and seventy-seven grain. I'm the humblest man at this table, Your Majesty, so forgive me if I bid on a pot or two. Reina, the Jar sits so nicely beside a Bowl, I know. I'd hate to make it expensive for you, but I'd hate even more to go home with nothing.",
 "Rich: I can't finish a set this year and still have the twenty for the seat, so this is about where the pieces go. Most of all I don't want Reina holding a Jar next to her Bowl while I've got nothing on my board.")
say("Reina", "Rich, darling, you need three pieces and I need one, so let's not make the Pile fat for nothing. You can have my Scroll and Paddy's Bowl without a peep from me, and I'll take the Jar. Bid against me on it and you'll only have less for the two you actually need.",
 "Reina: Fifty-two still leaves me twenty and a half, which is the twenty I need to change seats, so if I get the Jar I can take a chair before anyone else can sell one. Rich has to buy all three pieces, so every grain he puts into the Jar is a grain he won't have for my Scroll.")
say("Clark", "Rich, you need three pieces, I need three, and only one of us has a canal paying twenty a year from next harvest. I'll bid my honest number on the Scroll and the Bowl and not a grain of spite. The Jar opens at ten, Reina, and that's me being neighbourly.",
 "Clark: Rich has the bigger purse today, but I have the bigger income from next year, so I'll pay a fair price and let him spend himself thin on the rest. The Bowl is the one I really don't want him to have, because an Official with a full set is coming for someone's seat, and it's never Silke's.")
say("Paddy", "Reina, I'll not fatten the Pile chasing a Jar with thirty-two grain in my pocket, so it's yours as far as I'm concerned. The Scroll, though, was mine until Rob's lad carried it off, and I'd like it back. I'll bid it honestly, Rich, Clark, and no spite in it.",
 "Paddy: I can't outbid Reina's seventy-two for the Jar, so I've stood aside, and maybe she'll remember that. I'll spend nearly all I have on the Scroll, and next year's eighty-five-grain harvest can go after the next Jar.")
act("The Land Deed Scroll on the block is the one Silke returned to Province 3's stock; Paddy's own Scroll stays on O18 in Rob's desert.", map_hl=[3,8])
say("Rob", "Six years and not a grain spent on crockery, and I'm not breaking the habit for Clark's Jar. Reina, it's all yours if you want it, darling, but buy it knowing my W1 is three steps from your Bowl and your Customs House. A full set lasts exactly as long as it takes me to get there.",
 "Rob: Reina is one Jar from a full set and a seat further up the table, and that's why W1 is riding at her pieces. Let her pay the Pile for it, and I'll break it up for free.")
DH = "Too rich for a humble bookkeeper. Enjoy it, and do mind Rob's boots."
DC = "Enjoy it. I've written the price down, and I'll see that piece again on the board."
lot(3, "LD", [B(5,18), B(5,None,DC), B(2,22), B(2,None,DH), B(6,23)],
 hover=TH(("", "Reina's minimum was 10. Clark stopped at 18 and Rich at 22; Paddy would have gone to 30."),
          ("Paddy", "I have two pieces of the set now, side by side in the corner of my paddies furthest from Rob, so all I'm missing is a Dragon Jar. I've only nine grain left, but an eighty-five-grain harvest is on its way, and next year I'll be bidding with a full purse. (With his quarter of the Bowl, 4, he holds 13.)")), at="S24")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(2,40), B(2,None,DH), B(3,41)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 10. Rich would have gone to 30 for his set and to 40 to keep it from Reina; Reina would have gone to 52. Reina puts the Jar on M13.", at="M13")
lot(6, "WB", [B(2,15), B(2,None,DH), B(5,16)],
 hover=TH(("", "Paddy's minimum was 6. Rich stopped at 15; Clark would have gone to 16 for his set and to 19 to keep it from Rich."),
          ("Clark", "One piece down and two to go, and I have Rich's Official seat in my sights: he's sitting there with an empty board after losing three lots by a grain each. P26 is out of every soldier's reach for two years, so the Bowl will still be there when I've bought a Scroll and a Customs House to go beside it.")), at="P26")
say("Paddy", "Twenty-three to twenty-two, and Rich, I'm sorry, but for once the grain went my way. That's two times in a row now. It goes on S24, right beside my Customs House, so Rob, if your lads come calling, they'll find both down the far end of a long road.")
say("Clark", "Sixteen for a Bowl, a grain past Rich, and forty-one for my Jar, Reina, so the quarter coming back to me is ten. Thank you both for being so generous to a humble silk farmer. Rob, it sits down by my new squares on the far shore, so W2 will be wading.")
say("Reina", "Silke, darling, you did tell us that patience and a purse beat any promise, and I took notes. I'll have the throne back, with thanks for keeping it warm. The Gentry's chair is a lovely one; I've left the Harvest Pile in perfect order. And Rob, you won't need to walk to my Bowl now: it's going back to the stock, so your W1 can stay where it is.",
 "Reina: Three years ago Rich took the throne from me over a teacup, and today I've taken it back with a hundred and sixty-five grain of reserve, so two hundred and two and a half in all. Now I finish the Winter Palace's neighbours and dig the canals, and this time I'll pay Rob to stay away before anyone else can save up a set.")
swap(3, 1, "Reina, holding a full set (Customs House M10, Wooden Bowl M12, Dragon Jar M13) and 37 grain, changes seats with Silke and takes the throne back. She takes the crown's whole reserve, 165.5, for 202.5 in all. Becoming Empress returns all her pieces to their provinces' stock: the Customs House to Province 2, the Dragon Jar to Province 5, the Wooden Bowl to Province 6; they'll be auctioned next year. Silke moves to Province 3 as the Gentry, holding nothing, and draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
     hover="Nobody had bought a soldier, so none change hands. Silke's canal and Province 1's four new squares stand; the new Empress pays the year's salaries.")
march({"W1":"J13", "W2":"M25", "W3":"R19"}, text="Rob marches all three: W1 to J13 in Rich's Province 2, W2 to M25 in Clark's Province 5, W3 to R19 in Paddy's Province 6. Each raids an empty square: 5 each from Rich, Clark and Paddy. He hires no one; his army is at its limit.")
say("Rob", "Three Emperors in two years. I've never seen a chair so keen to be sat in. Welcome back, Reina; the palaces are yours again, which means every one I burn costs you twenty. Clark, \"wading\" is exactly right: W2 is four steps from your Bowl, and I'm telling you now so it's fair. Paddy, five for the road, and W3 is walking that long road of yours.",
 "Rob: Clark's Bowl is the nearest piece I can count toward my objective, so W2 takes the shore road to it, collecting rent on the way. W3 heads for Paddy's pair, and W1 turns south toward the Winter Palace, because Reina's reserve is the fattest purse here now.")
act("After the march, W2 on M25 is 3 rounds from Clark's Wooden Bowl on P26, not four.", map_hl=[5])
say("Reina", "Rich, ten for the book, the same as you were promised; I don't make the Official pay for my good fortune. Rob, you keep the cards, and I'm doubling it to ten. Call it the cost of a quiet road past my palaces, and do read me something gentle.",
 "Reina: Two palaces at twenty a raid makes Rob's ten the cheapest insurance in China, and W2 is five rounds from the Winter Palace. A bandit with a salary has a reason to burn somebody else's barns.")
salaries({2:10, 8:10}, text="Salaries: Reina, the Empress again, pays Rich 10 as the Official and keeps Rob on as the I Ching reader at 10, double his old pay.", hover="The Emperor owes the Official at least 5 a year; an appointee's pay is the Emperor's to choose.")

# ===== CHAPTER 4: A Grain Past Careful
# ============================== YEAR 7 ==============================
R(7, "Careless Prosperity", "careless")
act("Rob, as the Empress's appointed reader, turns the I Ching card: Careless Prosperity. Every province collects a good harvest, the population holds at x2, 200 million, and every player plays the next Personal Disaster in their booklet.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Rob", "\"Careless Prosperity. Good times make careless people.\" All provinces collect a good harvest, and every one of you plays a Personal Disaster, so turn to the next one in your booklet. The counter reads two hundred million, population times two, so read the x2 row. Do enjoy the harvest, friends. Careless people leave their bowls lying about, and Clark, yours is three steps from my man.",
 "Rob: The stars just told the whole table to get sloppy, and I read it out with a straight face. While they're busy with their disasters, W2 walks to Clark's Bowl, and the Empress pays me for the reading.")
harvest("Silke, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: a good year at x2.")
disaster(1, "Reina pays 4.")
disaster(2, "Rich pays 4.")
disaster(3, "Silke pays 5.")
disaster(5, "Clark pays 4.")
disaster(6, "Paddy pays 4.")
disaster(8, "Rob's men may raid nothing in Provinces 5, 6 and 7 this year. They may still walk there, and raid Provinces 1, 2 and 3.")
say("Silke", "Paying out: Reina eighty, Rich twenty, me twenty, Clark a hundred and twenty, Paddy a hundred and ten, Rob nothing, my condolences. Now, rent. Clark, that's a hundred and fifty-three and a half in your pocket and a hundred and twenty more out of my pile, so ten is cheap. Paddy, a hundred and fourteen plus a hundred and ten, and you're one Jar short of a set. Twenty, please. My pile fed those canals, and the landlord eats too.",
 "Silke: Paddy has a Customs House and a Scroll, and now he's got enough grain to buy that Jar twice over, so every grain I take off him is a grain he can't bid against me. I'll settle for five if I have to, but I want the whole table to hear me count it. (Clark's 153.5 and Paddy's 114 already include the harvest.)")
rents(5, note="Rent: Silke asks Clark for 10 and Paddy for 20. The 5 each that the Gentry may ask is paid now; the rest, 5 more from Clark and 15 more from Paddy, is a negotiation, and each peasant answers it at his next turn.")
say("Reina", "Thirty percent, friends. A good harvest should be shared, not skinned. Paddy, your second canal section goes in this year at the crown's expense, and any of you who wants new land gets it free: I'll pay for every square. Rich, ten again for the collecting, and Rob, ten for the reading and the quiet road. Prosperity is only careless if nobody spends it on anything that lasts.",
 "Reina: Each square I pay for and each canal I dig raises the tax base for good, so in two years this generosity pays for itself and the reserve never piles up into a prize for whoever buys the Jar. Clark still has one Jar beside the board, so I'm watching his purse more than Rob's soldiers.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 30%: 6 each from Rich and Silke on their 20, 36 from Clark on his 120 and 33 from Paddy on his 110, 81 in all; Rob's income is nothing. She'll dig the second section of the Grand Canal in Paddy's Province 6 from the crown's grain, pay Rich 10 and Rob 10 at the year's end, and pay the whole cost of every square of new land any province buys this year.", map_hl=[1,6])
say("Rich", "Thirty percent, Your Majesty, and the most generous thirty I've ever collected. Here's my own six on top of the basket to start it. Silke, six, thank you kindly. Clark, thirty-six. Paddy, thirty-three, and you can watch me count it. That's eighty-one to the grain for the crown, and Rob, I'll write down that you were asked.",
 "Rich: At thirty percent I keep ninety-two, which is the most I've had all game, and a gentle crown means a quiet treasury and no reason to look at the man carrying the basket. Clark and Paddy have the fat purses this year, so they're the ones I'll be bidding a grain past at the auction.")
taxes(.30, collector="Rich, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={6:1}, text="Public works: Reina digs the second section of the Grand Canal in Paddy's Province 6, at the crown's expense. With both sections, Paddy's harvest gains 20 a year from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 40, into the Harvest Pile.")
act("New land: the crown will pay the whole cost of every square any province buys this year. Clark's Province 5 may take the sea; Paddy's Province 6 the sea or the Warlord's land; the crown the sea; Provinces 2 and 3 only once the Wall above them stands.", map_hl=[1,5,6])
say("Reina", "Thank you, Storyteller, the fields are mine already, so I'll take to the sea instead. Four squares under the Summer Palace, G27 and H25 to H27. The crown drains its own marshes first, and then it pays for yours.",
 "Reina: Four squares covers me if the population card goes to x4, and I've kept them near the palace on the western side, away from Rob's man at M25. That's 80 out of a reserve of about 280, which still leaves plenty to fund everyone else's land. (They're on the eastern shore, beside the Summer Palace on G26.)")
buy_land(1, ["G27","H25","H26","H27"], {1:80}, "New land: Reina buys four squares of sea for Province 1, G27 and H25 to H27, for 80 from the crown's reserve. Each adds 20 to the crown's harvest from next year.")
say("Clark", "Your Majesty said every square, and I wrote it down with the date, so: seven squares of coast, N26 down to R28, a hundred and forty on the crown's account. I'm leaving K25 to M26 alone; they're too close to Rob's man to be worth Your Majesty's grain. And Silke, the five is paid as owed. The other five is under 'negotiations, pending', and that page has a lot of room on it.",
 "Clark: Free land pays twenty a year forever. I've taken the seven squares furthest from W2 and left the crown thirty-nine and a half grain lighter for the kindness. A peasant with a harvest this size is the one who buys the next set when it comes loose, and he doesn't hand it to anyone else. (The crown pays 140 for them.)")
buy_land(5, ["N26","N27","N28","O28","P28","Q28","R28"], {1:140}, "New land: Clark buys seven squares of sea for Province 5, N26 to N28 and O28 to R28; the crown pays all 140. Each adds 20 to his harvest from next year. He doesn't agree to Silke's extra 5 of rent, so it isn't paid.")
say("Paddy", "Your Majesty, I'll take you at your word, and thank you for it. Four squares out along the sea at the bottom, Q28, R28, S27 and S28, as far from the desert as my paddies run. I won't be greedy and ask for the ones by the sand. Rob would only come and trample them.",
 "Paddy: Free land is free land. Those four add eighty to every harvest from next year, and I've kept them on the coast, well away from Rob's lads. Every grain the crown spends on my fields is a grain I keep for that Dragon Jar in Clark's stock.")
buy_land(6, ["S27","S28"], {1:40}, "New land: Paddy buys two squares of sea for Province 6, S27 and S28; the crown pays all 40. Q28 and R28, which he also asked for, went to Clark first: land is settled in table order. He says nothing to Silke's request for 15 more rent, so it isn't paid.")
say("Paddy", "Clark beat me to the Q and R squares, fair's fair, he's ahead of me at the table. One more out by the sea, S29, and I'll pay most of it myself, Your Majesty. Your basket's looking thin, and I'd not have Rich and Rob go short at the year's end on my account.",
 "Paddy: The crown's nearly broke, so I'm asking for only a token five, and at twenty a year the square pays me back fast. That leaves me sixty-one, enough to bid a grain past careful on Clark's Dragon Jar and still keep the twenty I need to climb.")
buy_land(6, ["S29"], {6:15, 1:5}, "New land: Paddy buys a third square of sea for Province 6, S29, paying 15 himself and asking the crown for only 5. Each of his three new squares adds 20 to his harvest from next year.")
act("Storyteller's tip, to Rob, said to the whole table: Rich, Silke and Clark are the three you still need. Whatever Rich and Silke buy goes onto an unraided square of their own provinces, so a soldier already standing inside Province 2 or 3 before an auction is close to wherever a piece lands.", map_hl=[2,3,8])
act("The auctions: three pieces come up in province order. Rich's Customs House (Province 2), Clark's Dragon Jar (Province 5), Paddy's Wooden Bowl (Province 6). Each seller sets the minimum for their own lot; Rich, Silke, Clark, Paddy and Rob each give a limit on every lot at once, and the lots are sold in order. The Empress doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,5,6])
say("Rich", "My Customs House opens at ten, friends. It's a fine house, kept to the letter, the same as I keep everything. As for the Jar and the Bowl, I've lost three lots by a single grain, so this year I'm told the humble thing is to bid like a man who means it.",
 "Rich: Paddy needs only that Jar for his set, and with sixty-one he can't pay past forty-one and still keep his twenty, so at fifty-six I either have the Jar or I've left him too poor to use it. The Bowl goes the same way: at thirty-two Silke can have it, but she'll have to spend everything to get it.")
say("Silke", "Sixty-one grain, Paddy. Take twenty off for the chair and you've got forty-one for a Jar, and I can count to forty-one as well as you can. Rich, ninety-two and two pieces short, so don't pretend you're only here to watch.",
 "Silke: I can't build a whole set out of seventy-nine grain this year, so the Jar's the one that counts: whatever Paddy wants it for, he doesn't get it. I'll pick up the Customs House and the Bowl cheap if the table lets me, and if they don't, I've still got grain for next year.")
say("Clark", "Rich, I'm after your Customs House, and the man who dug my canal knows I'm good for it. Silke, if you want it you'll pay landlord's prices, and this time I'm not the tenant. My Jar opens at sixty-two. Paddy, I'm sure that's a coincidence, and I'm sure you'll write it down.",
 "Clark: Paddy's sixty-one grain and one Jar from a set, so the Jar opens one grain above what he has. That buys me a year to get the Customs House and a Scroll before I take his seat or Rich's. If it doesn't sell it comes back at two, but a year is all I'm asking for.")
say("Paddy", "Three times now I've lost a Dragon Jar by a single grain, and I'm not proud. Clark's Jar is the last piece of my set. Rich, Silke, if either of you would stand aside for it, I'll stay off any Customs House you're after and you'll have my word on the next one. Otherwise I'm bidding.",
 "Paddy: Forty-seven is my limit. That keeps a few grain back after the twenty I need to climb, and it's well past careful, because losing by one grain a fourth time would be more than a man can stand. If Rich or Silke want it more than that they can have it, and they'll be poorer for it.")
say("Rob", "Not a grain from me, as usual. Buy away, Rich, Silke, buy everything. Every piece you put on your own fields is one I don't have to go looking for. Think of me as the delivery man, just running the other way.",
 "Rob: Whatever Rich and Silke buy lands in Provinces 2 and 3, and those are the two pieces I still need for my set. The disaster keeps me out of 5, 6 and 7 this year anyway, so I'm happy to let them pay the Pile and put new targets right in front of W1.")
DS = "Take it, then. I'll be counting what it cost you."
lot(2, "CH", [B(3,12), B(3,None,DS), B(5,13)],
 hover=TH(("", "Rich's minimum was 10. Silke stopped at 12; Clark would have gone to 45, and to 58 to keep it from Silke."),
          ("Clark", "So that's the Bowl and the Customs House, and the only thing I still need is a Scroll. P23 is out of every soldier's reach for two rounds. Nobody would buy my Jar at sixty-two, which means Paddy is still one piece short, same as I am.")), at="P23")
act("Clark's Dragon Jar opens at 62, and nobody meets it: Rich would have gone to 45 (56 to keep it from Paddy), Paddy to 47, Silke to 25 (45 to keep it from Paddy). It stays in Province 5's stock and must come up again next year with the minimum at 2.", map_hl=[5])
lot(6, "WB", [B(3,15), B(3,None,DS), B(2,16)],
 hover=TH(("", "Paddy's minimum was 5. Silke stopped at 15; Rich would have gone to 26, and to 32 to keep it from Silke."),
          ("Rich", "G16 is the corner of Province 2 farthest from the rider on J13, so my one piece is out of reach for two years. One down, and Clark's Jar comes back next year at two, with nobody else on the table holding the grain I have. (Clark, 99.5, and Rob, 172, hold more than his 79.)")), at="G16")
say("Clark", "Thirteen grain for the Customs House, Rich, and your quarter's three. That's the cheapest favour you've ever done me, and I'm putting it under 'promises kept' anyway. It goes on P23, in the middle of my farms. Rob, you're welcome to look, from a distance.")
say("Rich", "Thank you, Paddy, a beautiful bowl, and it's going in the far corner of my fields where nobody's boots ever go. Silke, I won by a grain for once, and I promise I'll be insufferable about it for at least a week.")
march({"W1":"I14", "W2":"N24", "W3":"Q20"}, text="Rob marches all three: W1 to I14 in Rich's Province 2, raiding an empty square for 5; W2 to N24 in Clark's Province 5 and W3 to Q20 in Paddy's Province 6, raiding nothing (his disaster bars raids on Provinces 5 to 7 this year). He hires no one; his army is at its limit.")
say("Rob", "Rich, you said the far corner where nobody's boots ever go? Mine do. G16 is three steps from W1, and this one's just to say hello, five out of Province 2. Clark, a Customs House in the middle of your farms is lovely, but it's three steps from W2 and four from W3. I'll be looking at it from closer than a distance.",
 "Rob: The disaster keeps me out of Provinces 5, 6 and 7 this year, so W2 and W3 just walk up on Clark's new house, and W1 takes Rich's five on the way to his Bowl. Next year both their pieces are in reach, and that's two of my last three.")
act("After the march, W1 on I14 is 2 rounds from Rich's Wooden Bowl on G16; W2 on N24 is 2 rounds from both Clark's Customs House on P23 and his Wooden Bowl on P26, and W3 on Q20 is 3 from the Customs House.", map_hl=[2,5])
salaries({2:10, 8:10}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Rich 10 as the Official and Rob 10 as the I Ching reader, as she announced.", hover="The Emperor owes the Official at least 5 a year; an appointee's pay is the Emperor's to choose. The crown ends the year with 14.5 in its reserve.")
# ============================== YEAR 8 ==============================
R(8, "Bad Harvest", "dribble2")
act("Rob, as the Empress's appointed reader, turns the I Ching card: Bad Harvest. Every province collects a bad harvest, and the population holds at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Rob", "\"Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame.\" Every province collects a bad harvest. The counter still reads two hundred million, population times two, so read the x2 row, bad harvest column. Don't take it too hard, friends. My desert grows nothing in a good year either, and I've never gone hungry.",
 "Rob: A lean year leaves the others with thin purses and nothing spare for guards, and that's exactly when my men are two steps from Rich's Bowl and Clark's Customs House. The disaster's over, so this year the march raids again.")
harvest("Silke, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: a bad year at x2. Negative incomes are paid into the pile.")
say("Silke", "Paying out: Reina a hundred and ten, Clark two hundred and ten, Paddy a hundred and thirty, and Rich, Rob and I pay the rotten seed back in. Clark, that's three hundred and nine and a half in your pocket on a bad year, so twenty from you is a rounding error you can file under 'gratitude'. Paddy, a hundred and ninety-five, so ten. I'm the only one at this table whose harvest went backwards, and I'm the one running the pile.",
 "Silke: Forty-nine grain and my fields losing thirty a year: I'm the poorest seat at the table that isn't sitting in the desert, and Clark is one Scroll away from a set with three hundred grain behind him. The Jar comes up at two next year and Paddy has a hundred and ninety-five to bid with, so I'll squeeze him now, while all he can say back is no. (The Jar comes up at 2 this year.)")
rents(5, note="Rent: Silke asks Clark for 20 and Paddy for 10. The 5 each is paid now; the rest, 15 more from Clark and 5 more from Paddy, is a negotiation each answers at his next turn.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent, and I'll be plain about why: last year I paid for every square at this table and the basket came home with fourteen and a half in it. Clark, Paddy, your harvests are the only ones that grew, so you'll carry the crown this year. Rich, your fields went backwards, so the crown digs your irrigation and pays all thirty. Half of every new square is still on me.",
 "Reina: That Jar comes up next year at two grain, and Paddy needs only the Jar for a full set, so every grain I tax out of his purse now is one he can't use against my chair. Watering Rich's fields keeps my Official friendly, and he's the man most likely to outbid Paddy. (The Jar comes up this year; a peasant can't take the throne directly.)")
act("Reina declares a tax of 40%: 84 from Clark on his 210 and 52 from Paddy on his 130, 136 in all; Rich, Silke and Rob had negative incomes and owe nothing. She'll build the Province 2 Irrigation System from the crown's grain, pay Rich 10 and Rob 10 at the year's end, and pay half of every square of new land another province buys this year.", map_hl=[1,2,5,6])
say("Rich", "Forty percent, Your Majesty, and thank you for the irrigation. Province 2 will drink to the crown's health. Clark, eighty-four, please. Paddy, fifty-two. That's a hundred and thirty-six to the grain, and Rob, you're in the book as asked and refused, as usual.",
 "Rich: Clark is one Scroll from a full set with over two hundred in his pocket, so every grain the crown takes off him helps me. My Bowl is two rounds from Rob's rider, so next year I need the Jar at two and a Scroll fast. (The Jar comes up this year.)")
taxes(.40, collector="Rich, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Province 2 Irrigation System", 30, {1:30}, key="irr2", at="I15:J15", text="Public works: Reina builds the Province 2 Irrigation System on I15 and J15, at the crown's expense. Rich's harvest gains 10 a year from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 30, into the Harvest Pile. The crown may build a negotiated work alone when it pays the whole cost.")
act("New land: the crown will pay half of every square another province buys this year. Clark's Province 5 may take the sea; Paddy's Province 6 the sea or the Warlord's land; Provinces 2 and 3 only once the Wall above them stands.", map_hl=[5,6])
say("Clark", "Five more squares out along the bottom row, N29 to R29, half each as Your Majesty offered: fifty from me and fifty from the crown. Silke, the five is paid. The fifteen goes under 'gratitude', and that's a page I've never once written on. The land closest to Rob's man I'm leaving for the gulls.",
 "Clark: Each of those squares pays me back inside a year, and they're the ones furthest from W2. I'm still holding back a hundred and seventy for the Scroll and the chair, because the Jar comes up at two and I want my grain ready when a Scroll comes loose.")
buy_land(5, ["N29","O29","P29","Q29","R29"], {5:50, 1:50}, "New land: Clark buys five squares of sea for Province 5, N29 to R29, paying 50; the crown pays 50. He declines Silke's extra 15 of rent, so it isn't paid.")
say("Paddy", "Two more squares out on the water, R29 and S30, half each with the crown, thank you, Your Majesty. And Silke, I'm sorry about your fields, truly, but the five is paid and the other five stays in my pocket. I've a Jar to buy this year, and you know as well as I do what that costs.",
 "Paddy: Two squares for twenty of my own grain adds forty a year, and they're out on the sea where Rob can't get at them. That still leaves me a hundred and eighteen, the fattest purse I've had for a Dragon Jar, and this time I don't mean to lose it by a grain.")
buy_land(6, ["S30"], {6:10, 1:10}, "New land: Paddy buys S30 for Province 6, paying 10; the crown pays 10. R29, which he also asked for, went to Clark a moment before: land is settled in table order. He declines Silke's extra 5 of rent.")
say("Paddy", "Clark had the R29 square off me first, so I'll take R30 just below it, half and half again, Your Majesty. That's the corner of the sea, and I'll stop there before I'm digging rice into the ocean.",
 "Paddy: Ten of my grain buys twenty a year out where no soldier walks. I'm leaving the T squares by the desert to Rob's boots, and that leaves me a hundred and eighteen for the Jar.")
buy_land(6, ["R30"], {6:10, 1:10}, "New land: Paddy buys R30 for Province 6 in place of R29, paying 10; the crown pays 10.")
act("The auction: one lot this year, Clark's Dragon Jar (Province 5), unsold last year, so it opens at 2 and Clark can't hold it back again. Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob each give a limit. The Empress doesn't bid.", map_hl=[5])
say("Rich", "Paddy, you've lost three Jars by a grain and I've lost a few lots that way myself, so I won't pretend I'm only watching. I'm bidding, and if it goes to you, you'll have paid what it's worth.",
 "Rich: Paddy has a hundred and eighteen and needs only this Jar, so I can't outbid him, but I can make him pay thirty-five for what might have cost him two. Thirty-four is as high as I go and still keep my twenty for the set, because a Jar is no use to me if I can't afford to sit down.")
say("Silke", "A hundred and eighteen, Paddy, less twenty for the chair, so you can go to ninety-eight, and the whole table can do that sum. I can't, but every grain I make you pay goes into my pile, and the pile is mine to count.",
 "Silke: With fifty-nine grain I can't outbid Paddy for that Jar, so what I can do is make him pay for it, and if he blinks first at forty-five, the last piece of his set is sitting in my province instead of his. Either way, after this the man I'm watching is Clark: three hundred grain and one Scroll from a set.")
say("Paddy", "Three Jars I've lost by a single grain, friends, and I've been saving all year so it won't be four. Rich, Silke, I know you both need it and I'm sorry, but this time I'm bidding like a man who means it.",
 "Paddy: Sixty-one is a grain more than Silke holds and well past Rich's purse, so neither of them can top it, and I still keep the twenty I need to climb. I've been a grain short three times and I won't be a fourth. (Silke holds 59.)")
say("Rob", "Two grain for a Jar and still not a bid from me. I've never paid for a piece in my life, and I'm not starting on Clark's crockery. Rich, Silke, if one of you wins it, put it somewhere nice. I'll be along to admire it.",
 "Rob: If Rich or Silke buys that Jar it lands in Province 2 or 3, which is one more target for my last two names. If Paddy gets it, he climbs off a seat I've already finished with, so either way it costs me nothing.")
DR = "Congratulations, Paddy. Do watch the desert, it's closer than it looks."
DSk = "Enjoy it, Paddy. Forty-odd grain is a lot of rice to pay for a pot."
lot(5, "DJ", [B(2,34), B(2,None,DR), B(3,45), B(3,None,DSk), B(6,46)],
 hover="The Jar went unsold last year, so it opened at 2. Rich stopped at 34; Silke would have gone to 15 for herself and to 45 to keep it from Paddy; Paddy would have gone to 61. Clark's quarter is 11.5. Paddy puts the Jar on R23.", at="R23")
say("Paddy", "Well, I'll be. Four tries and a grain past careful, and there's my set. Silke, I'm sorry, truly. You've counted that pile better than anyone, but I've sat in the paddies eight years and I'd like a turn at the upper table. The Rice Peasant's seat is a good one, with three canal sections, sea land out to the corner and a hundred and ninety-five a year in an average year. I've left it in fine order for you.",
 "Paddy: I take the Gentry's seat, and with it Silke's grain and the Harvest Pile. The Scroll goes back to Province 3's stock, and the Customs House and Jar come with me to new squares up north. I sat down a rice farmer with thirty-seven grain, and I'm still not quite sure how I got here. (Province 6 has two canal sections.)")
swap(6, 3, "Paddy, holding a full set (Customs House S25, Land Deed Scroll S24, Dragon Jar R23) and 72 grain, changes seats with Silke and becomes the Gentry. He takes her 59, for 131 in all. His Land Deed Scroll, Province 3's own piece, goes back to its stock, to be auctioned next year; the Customs House and the Jar move with him to Province 3. Silke becomes the Rice Peasant in Province 6, with its canals and new land, and draws 60 from the pile.",
     hover="Nobody had bought a soldier, so none change hands. Paddy puts the Customs House on M9 and the Jar on M10.", at={"CH":"M9","DJ":"M10"})
say("Paddy", "I'll put them side by side again, M9 and M10, in the quiet corner of the Gentry's fields. Old habits. Rob, I know your lads like a long walk, so I've given them one.",
 "Paddy: Those two squares are as far as Province 3 goes from all three of Rob's soldiers, so they should stay safe for a couple of years while I find a Wooden Bowl. Now I've a Harvest Pile to run and a whole new set of fields to worry about.")
march({"W1":"H15", "W2":"O23", "W3":"P21"}, text="Rob marches all three: W1 to H15 in Rich's Province 2, raiding an empty square for 5; W2 to O23 and W3 to P21 in Clark's Province 5, raiding two empty squares for 5 each. He hires no one; his army is at its limit.")
say("Rob", "Rich, your Bowl's in the far corner, and W1 is one step from it now, with five out of Province 2 for the walk. Clark, two men, five grain, one step from your Customs House. I named you last year, so nobody can call this a surprise. And congratulations, Paddy. The quiet corner is lovely. I'll be up to see it.",
 "Rob: Next round W1 takes Rich's Bowl and W2 or W3 takes Clark's Customs House, which leaves me one name short. Paddy's new set is the real threat now, one Bowl from the throne, so W1 has more work up north after Rich.")
act("After the march, W1 on H15 is 1 round from Rich's Wooden Bowl on G16; W2 on O23 is 1 round and W3 on P21 2 rounds from Clark's Customs House on P23. Clark paid 5 for each of the two raids, 10 in all.", map_hl=[2,5])
salaries({2:10, 8:10}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Rich 10 as the Official and Rob 10 as the I Ching reader, as she announced.", hover="The Emperor owes the Official at least 5 a year; an appointee's pay is the Emperor's to choose.")

# ===== CHAPTER 5: Two Scrolls in the Sand
# ============================== YEAR 9 ==============================
R(9, "Population Doubles", "double2")
act("Rob, as the Empress's appointed reader, turns the I Ching card: Population Doubles. Every province collects a good harvest, and the population doubles to x4, 400 million: every province now eats four times what it ate in the first year.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Rob", "\"Population Doubles. Fat years make large families, and every province gets a good harvest.\" The counter goes to four hundred million, so that's the x4 row on your income tables, everyone. Twice as many mouths, twice as many granaries, and every one of them on my road. Clark, Rich, I'd count your bowls tonight while you still have them.",
 "Rob: A good harvest fills every purse at this table, and mine's already the fattest, so the stars have finally learned whose side they're on. W1 is one step from Rich's Bowl and W2 is one step from Clark's Customs House. This march takes both, and then only Silke is left on my list.")
act("Storyteller's tip, to Rob, said to the whole table: the Jar and the Deed on your desert are yours to sell. The Warlord may sell raided realia privately, to anyone, and keeps the whole price, and the buyer places it at once on a square of their own province.", map_hl=[8])
harvest("Paddy, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: a good year at x4. Negative incomes are paid into the pile.")
say("Paddy", "Right, the pile pays out as the cards say: Reina forty, Clark two hundred and eighty, Silke a hundred and forty, and the rest of us pay in, me included, sixty out of my own purse. Rent's five apiece, as is owed. Clark, you've just had two hundred and eighty off a doubled harvest, so I'm asking you for twenty, not five. Silke, I know I turned you down twice when you asked me for more, so I'll not push you past ten. And if anyone at this table has a Wooden Bowl they'd part with, I'm listening, and I'll pay a fair price for it.",
 "Paddy: That harvest leaves me eleven grain, so the rent is the only thing feeding me now. If Clark gives me twenty I'm back over the twenty I need, and then all I'm missing is one Bowl, which Clark and Rich both have and Rob's lads might soon be carrying. (After the harvest Paddy holds 71.)")
rents(5, note="Rent: Paddy asks Clark for 20 and Silke for 10. The 5 each is paid now; the rest, 15 more from Clark and 5 more from Silke, is a negotiation each answers at their next turn.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent again, and again it falls on the two harvests that doubled. Clark, two hundred and eighty is a fine year, and the crown is only asking for its share of it. This year I'm putting up the whole Wall above my province, all four links, so the north-east stops being one long invitation. I'll pay half of any square anyone buys, and the sum is half, so nobody can say I promised more. Rich, your ten. Rob, your ten, and you can keep your eyes on the bowls as long as your hands stay off my palaces.",
 "Reina: The Wall is a hundred and sixty grain of stone that nobody can inherit and Rob can't walk through, and it still leaves me close to two hundred for the one thing I truly need this year. If any Wooden Bowl comes loose from that board, I'll be the one buying it, never Paddy. (To Rob: If your men happen to lift a Wooden Bowl this year, sell it to me and not to Paddy. Whatever he offers you, I'll pay more, from the crown, the same day, and nobody at the table needs to hear the price.)")
act("Reina declares a tax of 40%: 112 from Clark on his 280 and 56 from Silke on her 140, 168 in all; Rich, Paddy and Rob had negative incomes and owe nothing. She'll build all four links of the Great Wall above Province 1 from the crown's grain, pay Rich 10 and Rob 10 at the year's end, and pay half of every square of new land another province buys this year.", map_hl=[1,5,6])
say("Rich", "A hundred and twelve from Clark and fifty-six from Silke, a hundred and sixty-eight to the crown, Your Majesty, and I've checked it to the half million. Forty percent of two hundred and eighty is a hundred and twelve, Clark, so please don't hand me a hundred and ten and call it rounding. Paddy, my Bowl is the only thing I own in this world, and you'll forgive a poor Official for keeping hold of it while the Warlord is shopping his Jar and his Deed around the table.",
 "Rich: Nine grain. I can't buy so much as a sack until Reina pays me my ten, so the honest collector is all I've got left to sell. The Jar and Deed on Rob's desert are the two pieces I'm missing, and if Clark buys that Deed off him, the game's over before I can even make him pay for it. (A full set lets Clark change seats; the game ends only when every work is built.)")
taxes(.40, collector="Rich, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Great Wall, all four links above Province 1", 160, {1:160}, wall={1:4}, text="Public works: Reina builds all four links of the Great Wall above Province 1, at the crown's expense. Province 1's strip below the Wall is now open for new land.", hover="Reina pays the 160, into the Harvest Pile. Any number of links of one work count as one project.")
act("New land: the crown will pay half of every square another province buys this year. Clark's Province 5 may take the sea; Silke's Province 6 the sea or the Warlord's land; Province 1 the sea or, now its Wall stands, its strip; Provinces 2 and 3 only once the Wall above them stands.", map_hl=[1,5,6])
say("Reina", "The Wall's barely set and I'm already ploughing behind it: two squares at E21 and E22, twenty each, into the pile. Rich, you can check it to the half million. And the half-share offer stands for everyone else at this table, so buy your land before the Warlord's road gets any longer.",
 "Reina: Two squares is enough to keep my harvest ahead of the mouths, and it leaves about a hundred and forty-eight in the reserve, which covers everyone's half-shares and still leaves a real bid if a Wooden Bowl comes loose. Buying land behind a wall I've just built is the cheapest protection at this table. (The Emperor doesn't bid at auction; a Bowl could reach her only by private trade.)")
buy_land(1, ["E21","E22"], {1:40}, "New land: Reina buys E21 and E22 on Province 1's strip below the new Wall, at the crown's cost, 40.")
say("Clark", "Nine squares, Your Majesty, a hundred and eighty in all, ninety from me and ninety from the crown, which is half to the grain. I've written down that you said it was half and not a grain more, so neither of us has to remember it. And Paddy, you'll have your extra fifteen, the whole twenty, under 'goodwill toward the man holding the Scroll'. Rob, count my bowls whenever you like. I've only got the one, and I count it every night.",
 "Clark: The crown pays half of every square, and every square earns its whole price back next year, so that's free money with a palace stamp on it. I've still got two hundred and thirty-odd left after this, enough to outbid anyone here for Paddy's Scroll, or Rob's Deed if he's selling. (To Rob: Your Deed on O18 is no use to you sitting on the sand. I'll give you sixty for it now, privately, and I'll set it out of your soldiers' reach so neither of us has to argue about it again.)")
buy_land(5, ["M26","M27","M28","M29","M30","N30","O30","P30","Q30"], {5:90, 1:90}, "New land: Clark buys nine squares of sea for Province 5, M26 to M30 and N30 to Q30, paying 90; the crown pays 90.")
act("Clark pays Paddy the extra 15 of rent he asked for, 20 in all this year.", deltas={2:-15, 6:15}, deal=True)
say("Silke", "Four squares at the far end of the coast, Your Majesty: eighty to the pile, your half is forty and mine's forty, and I'll hold you to 'the sum is half.' Paddy, you get the five you're owed and not a grain more, exactly as you taught me. And Rob, I can count too: your men are at O23 and P21, so I'm building where they'll need a long walk.",
 "Silke: Eighty more a year from next harvest for forty out of my own purse, and none of it within a soldier's reach this year, so I keep ninety-nine in hand for crockery. Rob's selling a Jar and a Deed off his desert, and I'll be the one who buys them when I've got somewhere safe to put them.")
buy_land(6, ["T27","T28","T29","T30"], {6:40, 1:40}, "New land: Silke buys four squares for Province 6, T27 to T30, paying 40; the crown pays 40. She declines Paddy's extra 5 of rent, so it isn't paid.")
act("The auction: one lot this year, Paddy's Land Deed Scroll (Province 3), returned to stock when he changed seats last year. Paddy sets its minimum; Rich, Clark, Silke and Rob each give a limit. The Empress doesn't bid.", map_hl=[3])
say("Paddy", "Thank you, Clark, that's very decent of you, and I've written it down too. Silke, five it is, fair's fair, I did teach you that. Now, the Land Deed Scroll from my old province is up, and I'll start it at ten. It's a good piece and it sits as far from Rob's road as the north-west goes, so whoever's still missing one, there it is.",
 "Paddy: I don't need the Scroll any more, and I only get a quarter of whatever it fetches, so ten is a fair floor without scaring anyone off. What I'm really watching is who goes for it, because whoever wants a Deed is short of a set, and I'd like to know who's coming up the table behind me.")
say("Rich", "Nine grain is everything I have, Paddy, and every grain of it goes on your Scroll. Clark, a man with two hundred and thirty can afford to pay a poor Official's whole purse plus one, and I'll applaud when he does. Your Majesty, take note of who pays the most for that Scroll, because the man who completes a set this year isn't buying it to carry your basket.",
 "Rich: I said I'd make the Scroll cost Clark everything my purse could push, and my purse pushes exactly nine. So the best I can do now is make sure Reina is watching her throne when he climbs the steps.")
say("Clark", "Paddy, I paid your twenty in full, and the book has it under 'goodwill toward the man holding the Scroll', so you know which page I'm reading from. Silke, you've got ninety-nine and I've got two hundred and thirty. I'd rather you stood aside and kept your grain than made the pile richer and both of us poorer. Your page in the book has room for 'sensible'.",
 "Clark: I've sworn for three years to stop furnishing other people's thrones, and this is the last piece of my own. I'll go to two hundred and five, which still leaves me the twenty the set needs, and nobody at this table can follow me that far.")
say("Silke", "Clark, you've got two hundred and thirty and that Scroll finishes your set, so I know who's going home with it. I'll bid what it's worth to me and not a grain more. Paddy, three quarters of whatever he pays goes into your pile, so don't look so pleased with yourself.",
 "Silke: Thirty is my honest price for the first of three pieces, and if Clark wants it more he'll pay a lot more, and that's all going into the pile I get paid from. I'm not bidding to stop anybody this time, I'm only building my own set.")
say("Rob", "Pass, as ever. I've never paid for a piece in my life, and I'm not starting with Paddy's paperwork. And since we're all such good friends, Clark has just offered me sixty, privately, for the Deed on my sand. It's not for sale, Clark. Pieces in my desert stay there, and the ones in your province are only waiting to join them.",
 "Rob: That Deed counts toward my set, so sixty grain won't buy it, and reading Clark's whisper out sets him and Paddy watching each other over the Scroll. Reina's quiet offer for a Bowl I'll keep to myself for now, because a woman with eighteen grain in the treasury doesn't get to outbid anyone. (A raid counts once it's made: selling the raided Deed wouldn't undo it.)")
DSk9 = "Take it, Clark. That's a fine price, and three quarters of it comes back to my coast at the next harvest."
lot(3, "LD", [B(6,30), B(6,None,DSk9), B(5,31)],
 hover="Paddy set the minimum at 10, so Rich's 9 didn't bid; Silke stopped at 30; Clark would have gone to 205. Paddy's quarter is 7.5. Clark puts the Scroll on O26.", at="O26")
say("Clark", "Rich, you promised Province 5 a canal in Year 4, and you kept it, so it's only fair you get to farm beside it. The coast is yours, all two hundred and thirty-odd squares of sea, and I'll take the ledger back. I'm the Official again, Your Majesty, and I've kept a very careful count of this crown's tax. Paddy, your Scroll did exactly what you said it would.",
 "Clark: Rob's man is one step from my Customs House, so a set I sit on is a set I lose. I'm taking the weakest chair at the table, the one I've wanted since Year 4, and from the Official's desk the throne is only one more set away.")
swap(5, 2, "Clark, holding a full set (Wooden Bowl P26, Customs House P23, Land Deed Scroll O26) and 199 grain, changes seats with Rich and becomes the Official again. He takes Rich's 9, for 208 in all. His Customs House, Province 2's own piece, goes back to its stock, to be auctioned next year; the Bowl and the Scroll move with him to Province 2. Rich becomes the Silk Peasant in Province 5, with its canals and 25 squares of sea land; his Wooden Bowl goes back to Province 6's stock, and he draws 60 from the pile.",
     hover="Nobody had bought a soldier, so none change hands. Province 5 has 25 squares of new land, not 230 (230 was Clark's grain before the auction).", at={"WB":"J16","LD":"J14"})
say("Clark", "Twenty-five squares of sea, then, thank you, Storyteller. It's still the best farm at this table, Rich, and the book will show you got it for nine grain. As for me, I need a Dragon Jar, and there are three on this board and one in the desert. Rob, I've noticed you don't sell, but I've also noticed you never stop talking about prices.",
 "Clark: I'm putting them on J14 and J16, which are as far from W1 as Province 2 goes this year, though nothing here is safe next year, so the Jar has to come quickly. Reina's down to eighteen and a half grain on the throne, and a broke Emperor is the softest seat in the kingdom. (There are two Jars on the board: Paddy's on M10 and Rob's on T17.)")
march({"W1":"I15", "W2":"N23", "W3":"O21"}, text="Rob marches all three: W1 to I15 in Clark's Province 2, crossing the Irrigation System (a 5 toll to Clark) and raiding an empty square for 5; W2 to N23 and W3 to O21 in Rich's Province 5, raiding two empty squares for 5 each. He hires no one; his army is at its limit.")
say("Rob", "Clark, you took your crockery north and put it right next to my man. That isn't hiding, that's setting the table. W1 pays his two at your irrigation gate like an honest traveller and takes five while he's there, and next march he's beside your Bowl and your Scroll both. Rich, my other two are just passing through your new farm on their way west. Five each. Consider it a welcome to the neighbourhood.",
 "Rob: Every name on my list keeps climbing just before I get there, so this time I'm parking W1 where Clark's Bowl and Scroll are both one step away, and he can't move two pieces at once. W2 and W3 are turning west toward the Winter Palace, and every palace they burn costs Reina twenty she hasn't got. (The toll at the Irrigation System is 5.)")
say("Reina", "I announced ten each, and the reserve holds eighteen and a half, so here is the honest arithmetic before anyone writes it down for me. Clark, you get eight and a half, which is every grain the crown has left after Rob's ten. Count it as a debt of honour and not of grain. Rob, your ten, in full and on time, because my palaces would like another quiet year.",
 "Reina: I spent the year on a Wall and half the coast's land, and I'm ending it at exactly zero, which is empty but isn't debt, so it gives nobody a revolt clause. I shorted Clark because Rob's ten is what keeps the Winter Palace standing, and with W1 sitting beside Clark's Bowl, I'd rather the Warlord remembered which of us pays him. (A reserve too low is itself a cause for revolt, though nobody has called one.)")
salaries({2:8.5, 8:10}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 8.5 as the Official and Rob 10 as the I Ching reader. The reserve is empty.", hover="The Emperor owes the Official at least 5 a year; an appointee's pay is the Emperor's to choose.")
# ============================== YEAR 10 ==============================
R(10, "Population Doubles", "double3")
act("Rob, as the Empress's appointed reader, turns the I Ching card: Population Doubles again. The population has already doubled twice, so only the good harvest counts: every province collects a good harvest, and the population holds at x4, 400 million.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Rob", "\"Population Doubles,\" again. But we've already doubled twice, so the card only gives us the good harvest. The counter stays at four hundred million, x4, same row as last year. Fat fields everywhere. Your Majesty, thank you for my ten. It's touching that the crown's last grain went to the stars and the road and not to its own Official. Clark, you'll want to look at your Bowl and your Scroll tonight. My man at I15 is close enough to read them.",
 "Rob: The crown's reserve is empty, so the next palace I burn is twenty Reina has to find somewhere she doesn't have it, and that's how a throne starts to wobble. W1 is one step from both of Clark's pieces, so whichever one he runs with, I take the other.")
harvest("Paddy, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: a good year at x4. Negative incomes are paid into the pile.")
say("Paddy", "The pile pays as the cards say: Rich four hundred and sixty, Silke two hundred and twenty, Reina eighty, and Clark, Rob and I pay in, sixty from me. Rent's five apiece as owed. Rich, you came to that coast with nine grain and you've five hundred and ten now, so I'm asking you for forty and I think that's modest. Silke, a fine year deserves fifteen, and I'll not be cross if you say no again. I'm saving every grain for next year, because I hear there's a Wooden Bowl going back in Province 6's stock.",
 "Paddy: Rich's old Bowl went back to Province 6's stock when he changed seats, and that Bowl is the last thing I'm missing, so whatever rent I squeeze out this year is my bid for it. I'm down to forty-three and a half, and I'd like to walk into that auction with a good deal more. (The Bowl is auctioned this year.)")
rents(5, note="Rent: Paddy asks Rich for 40 and Silke for 15. The 5 each is paid now; the rest, 35 more from Rich and 10 more from Silke, is a negotiation each answers at their next turn.")
say("Reina", "Forty percent again, and it falls only on the harvests that grew. Rich, a hundred and eighty-four out of four hundred and sixty still leaves you the richest farmer in China, and Silke, eighty-eight out of two hundred and twenty. This year the crown puts up the Marble Pagoda, so there'll be something beautiful in the north-east as well as a Wall. Clark, your full ten this time, and the half grain I owed you is paid with interest. And the half-share on new land stands, the sum is half, for every square anyone buys.",
 "Reina: The Pagoda costs twenty, so it's the cheapest monument there is, and it keeps three hundred and more in the reserve for everyone's land halves, so nobody can call me stingy and point to the revolt clause. Paying Clark the whole ten matters more than the grain: he's the one with a Bowl and a Scroll, hunting for a Jar, and I want him writing 'paid in full' in that book of his.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 40%: 184 from Rich on his 460 and 88 from Silke on her 220, 272 in all; Clark, Paddy and Rob had negative incomes and owe nothing. She'll build the Marble Pagoda from the crown's grain, buy two squares of new land for Province 1, pay Clark 10 at the year's end, and pay half of every square another province buys this year.", map_hl=[1,5,6])
say("Clark", "A hundred and eighty-four from Rich and eighty-eight from Silke, two hundred and seventy-two to the crown, Your Majesty, checked to the half million. Rich, forty percent of four hundred and sixty is a hundred and eighty-four, so don't hand me a hundred and eighty and call it rounding; I learned that line from a very good teacher. And while I have the table's attention, I'm buying a Dragon Jar, a hundred grain, cash, to whoever sells. Rob, Paddy, that offer is in the book with today's date.",
 "Clark: I count it honestly because an honest count is the only thing an Official can sell, and Reina will need every grain of it if she means to hold the throne. A hundred for a Jar is a lot of grain, but one more Jar is all that stands between me and that throne, and I'd rather pay it than wait for Rob's walk.")
taxes(.40, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Marble Pagoda", 20, {1:20}, key="Marble Pagoda", at="F17", text="Public works: Reina builds the Marble Pagoda on F17, at the crown's expense, the year's one project.", hover="Reina pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. The Pagoda goes where the full game put it.")
buy_land(1, ["D21","D22"], {1:40}, "New land: Reina buys D21 and D22 on Province 1's strip, at the crown's cost, 40.")
act("New land: the crown will pay half of every square another province buys this year. Rich's Province 5 may take the sea; Silke's Province 6 the sea or the Warlord's land; Provinces 2 and 3 only once the Wall above them stands.", map_hl=[5,6])
say("Rich", "Seven squares, Your Majesty, K25 and K26 and L26 through L30, a hundred and forty to the pile. Seventy from me and seventy from the crown, which is half to the grain, and I have it in writing that the sum is half. Paddy, you'll have your five, as owed, with my warmest regards, but a man who came to the coast with nine grain will be forgiven for remembering how it felt.",
 "Rich: Each square costs me ten and pays twenty even if the game ends next year, so the crown is paying me to farm, and I'll bow to her for every one of them. The rest of the purse stays shut, because this year the Bowl and the Customs House both come up, and Paddy's forty would only go to outbidding me for the Bowl.")
buy_land(5, ["K25","K26","L26","L27","L28","L29","L30"], {5:70, 1:70}, "New land: Rich buys seven squares of sea for Province 5, K25, K26 and L26 to L30, paying 70; the crown pays 70. He declines Paddy's extra 35 of rent, so it isn't paid.")
say("Silke", "Seven more squares down the coast, Your Majesty: a hundred and forty to the pile, seventy from me and seventy from the crown, and the sum is half, just as you said. Paddy, you'll have your five and that's all, and no, I'm not cross either. Rich, five hundred and ten less a hundred and eighty-four is three hundred and twenty-six, so I'd keep a close eye on those two soldiers walking across your farm.",
 "Silke: Each square costs me ten and pays twenty even if the game ends next year, which is the best price at this table, and it all sits at the far end of the coast, a long march from Rob's men. I've still got a hundred and fifty-six put by for the Customs House when it comes up.")
buy_land(6, ["T25","T26","U26","U27","U28","U29","U30"], {6:70, 1:70}, "New land: Silke buys seven squares for Province 6, T25, T26 and U26 to U30, paying 70; the crown pays 70. She declines Paddy's extra 10 of rent, so it isn't paid.")
act("The auction: two lots this year, Clark's Customs House (Province 2), returned when he changed seats, and Silke's Wooden Bowl (Province 6), returned when Rich did. Each seller sets a minimum; the others give limits. The Empress doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,6])
say("Clark", "My Customs House opens at fifty. Rich, you've two hundred and fifty-one and an empty board, and you know better than anyone what a Customs House is worth, since you sold me this one for thirteen. Silke's Bowl I leave to you and Paddy. The book will record who paid what, and who finished a set off whose pottery.",
 "Clark: I need every grain I've got for a Dragon Jar and the twenty that goes with it, so I'm not bidding against anyone this year. I'll let Rich's farm money buy my Customs House at my price and take a quarter of it, which is the first time my own lot has paid me instead of putting somebody else on a throne.")
say("Paddy", "I'll be plain, since I'm no good at the other thing: that Bowl is the last piece I need, and I've fifty-three and a half to my name. Rich, you've two hundred and fifty-one, and you need a Customs House as well as the Bowl, so here's a public offer. I'll stay out of the Customs House altogether, and if you leave the Bowl to me, I'll ask you for nothing past the five as long as I run the pile, and I give you my word I'll not come for the Silk Peasant's seat. Clark, thank you, but my Jar isn't for sale. It took me four tries.",
 "Paddy: Thirty-three is as far as I can go and still keep the twenty grain the set needs, so if Rich wants that Bowl he can have it, and I can't stop him. I've offered him all I've got, which is my word, a light rent and a clear run at the Customs House, and I'm hoping a man who knows what it is to be down to nine grain will take it.")
say("Rich", "Clark, Silke, I've bid a grain short three times at this table and written it in my diary as a sin, so this year I'm bidding like a farmer who's finally had a good harvest. Paddy, you're welcome to the Bowl if your purse is longer than mine, and I'll congratulate you very warmly if it is.",
 "Rich: Paddy has fifty-three and a half and needs only that Bowl to finish his set, so sixty shuts him out without starting a war. Silke gets the Customs House only if she'll pay more than a hundred and ten, which would leave her purse too thin to climb anywhere.")
say("Silke", "Rich, you've two hundred and fifty-one and I've a hundred and fifty-six, so you can have the Customs House if you want it badly enough, but I'll make you pay for it. And my Bowl starts at thirty, Paddy. That's not aimed at you, it's what a Bowl costs, and you've got fifty-three and a half, so you can afford it.",
 "Silke: The Customs House is the first piece of my set and it sits safe at R22, so sixty is my limit, and anything above that just fills the pile. I've stopped trying to keep Paddy down; thirty on my Bowl is a fair price and a quarter of it comes to me.")
say("Rob", "Pass and pass. Still not a grain on crockery after ten years. Clark, your hundred is very handsome, and it's in your book with today's date, but my Jar stays in the sand. I'm not selling you the last piece you need while my man is standing one step from the two you've got. Rich, Paddy, bid each other up on that Bowl as hard as you like. Three quarters goes to the pile, and I'll pass the pile on my way.",
 "Rob: If I sold Clark that Jar he'd finish his set and climb, his Bowl and Scroll would go back into stock, and W1 would have walked all that way for nothing. This way he's stuck in his seat with two pieces one step from my man, and next march I take one of them.")
DSk10 = "Go on, then, Rich. Three quarters of that comes back to my coast at the next harvest."
lot(2, "CH", [B(6,60), B(6,None,DSk10), B(5,61)],
 hover="Clark set the minimum at 50. Silke stopped at 60; Rich would have gone to 70, and to 110 to keep it from Silke. Clark's quarter is 15. Rich puts the Customs House on L25.", at="L25")
DP10 = "Well. Five tries, then. I've been one grain short before, and I'll be at the next one."
lot(6, "WB", [B(3,33), B(3,None,DP10), B(5,34)],
 hover="Silke set the minimum at 30. Paddy stopped at 33, all he could spend and keep the 20 a set needs; Rich would have gone to 60. Silke's quarter is 8.5. Rich puts the Bowl on P26.", at="P26")
say("Rich", "Clark, thank you for the Customs House, and I mean that as sincerely as you meant it when I sold you one for thirteen. Paddy, I'm truly sorry. My limits were set before your offer reached me, and a grain over your purse is a cruel way to lose, I know that better than anyone here. As for where I've put them, I've learned my lesson and I'll say nothing about the boots.",
 "Rich: One on the far tip of the new land and one tucked down the coast, as far apart as my two soldiers' worth of trouble allows, and this time I said nothing about where. I need only a Scroll now, and Rob is sitting on one in his desert. (New land can't take a piece, so the Customs House went on L25 instead.)")
say("Rich", "My apologies, Storyteller, the far tip of the sea isn't the province's own soil yet, so the Customs House goes on proper old ground. And three more squares of sea, Your Majesty, sixty to the pile, thirty from me and thirty from the crown, half to the grain as always.",
 "Rich: That's three pieces of the set, and the only Scroll left in China is Rob's, sitting in his sand, so I'm keeping most of my purse for the day he finally names a price. Three more squares at ten apiece is the cheapest harvest anyone's offered me. (He holds two of the three; Clark's Scroll on J14 is the other Land Deed on the board.)")
buy_land(5, ["J25","J26","J27"], {5:30, 1:30}, "New land: Rich buys three more squares of sea for Province 5, J25 to J27, paying 30; the crown pays 30.")
march({"W1":"J14", "W2":"M24", "W3":"P22"}, take={"W1":("LD","M15")}, text="Rob marches: W1 steps onto J14 in Clark's Province 2 and takes his Land Deed Scroll instead of the 5, setting it on M15 in the desert. Clark's name comes off Rob's list. W2 steps to M24, a raided square; W3 to P22 in Rich's Province 5, raiding him for 5. He hires no one.")
say("Rob", "I told you, Clark. Whichever one you didn't move, I'd take, and you didn't move either. Your Land Deed Scroll goes into my sand at M15. That's three names down to two. Rich, you need a Scroll, and the only two left in China are both standing in my desert, so the price of climbing just went up. My men on your farm send their regards. One of them is two steps from that Customs House you weren't going to talk about.",
 "Rob: I took the Scroll rather than the Bowl, because the Scroll is the one piece Rich needs, and now both Scrolls on the board are mine. He can't climb, so his Customs House and Bowl stay put for W2 and W3 to come and collect.")
say("Reina", "Clark, your ten in full, as I said. Rob, twenty this year, because ten years of reading the stars and leaving my palaces alone has earned a raise, and I'd like the stars to stay this kind. And two more squares behind my Wall, C21 and C22, so the north-east feeds itself whatever the next card says.",
 "Reina: Both Scrolls left in China are in Rob's sand, and Rich needs only one of them to climb, so I've doubled the bandit's pay, which is cheaper than losing the throne. The whisper is the other half of the price. (To Rob: That extra ten isn't charity. As long as neither of your Land Deed Scrolls leaves the desert for Rich, or for anyone but me, the reader's pay stays at twenty every year I'm on the throne. If anyone makes you an offer for a Scroll, tell me the number first and I'll beat it from the crown.)")
buy_land(1, ["C21","C22"], {1:40}, "New land: Reina buys C21 and C22 on Province 1's strip, at the crown's cost, 40.")
salaries({2:10, 8:20}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 10 as the Official and Rob 20 as the I Ching reader.", hover="The Emperor owes the Official at least 5 a year; an appointee's pay is the Emperor's to choose.")

# ===== CHAPTER 6: Grain Doesn't Sit in a Chair
# ============================== YEAR 11 ==============================
R(11, "Utility Tax", "utility")
act("Rob, as the Empress's appointed reader, turns the I Ching card: the Utility Tax. Every province collects an average harvest; then each of Provinces 2, 3, 5 and 6 pays the Empress 2, and 2 more where it has a public work. The population holds at x4, 400 million.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Rob", "\"Utility Tax. A new levy, for the upkeep of things you may or may not have.\" Average harvest for everyone. Population holds at 400 million, so it's the x4 row on your tables. Every province pays the crown 2, and any province with a Public Works pays 2 more. Don't look at me, I haven't got any works, and I'm not paying tax on the ones I'm about to burn. Rich, W2 is one step from your Customs House. You'll want to pay for its upkeep quickly, while you've still got it.",
 "Rob: The stars want two grain from every province for the crown's upkeep, and I'm about to make the crown's upkeep a great deal more expensive. Rich is the name this march, then Silke, and then the chair.")
act("Storyteller's tip, to Silke, said to the whole table: there's no stock left, so every piece now changes hands by private trade. Both Land Deeds and one of the two Jars stand on Rob's desert, and he may sell them; Paddy holds the other Jar.", map_hl=[6,8])
sell_back(pid(3), ["CH","DJ"], "Paddy's own harvest is -85 this year and he has 53.5, so before the pile is paid he must sell realia back to it at 2 each (Note 7): his Customs House from M9 and his Dragon Jar from M10. Both go into the Harvest Pile's stock, to be auctioned this year after the provinces' lots.", hover="Four grain from both pieces doesn't cover the 85; the rest is a debt to the pile. In debt he can't bid, buy, trade, give grain or change seats, and every bag he receives pays the debt first. Debt is a cause for revolt.")
harvest("Paddy, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: an average year at x4. Negative incomes are paid into the pile.")
act("The Utility Tax: Clark (Province 2), Rich (Province 5) and Silke (Province 6) each pay the Empress 4, the levy and 2 more for their works; Paddy (Province 3, no works) pays 2. The crown takes 14.", deltas={pid(1):14, pid(2):-4, pid(3):-2, pid(5):-4, pid(6):-4}, map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6])
say("Paddy", "Rich, Silke, it's five each, same as always, and I'll not pretend I'm in a position to ask for more. Though if either of you fancied making it fifteen, with seven hundred and fifty and five hundred in your sacks, I'd remember it a long while. I sold my Jar and my Customs House back to the pile for two grain each tonight, the two pieces it took me four tries and a grain past careful to get, so I know exactly what being careful costs.",
 "Paddy: Ten grain of rent takes me from twenty-nine and a half under to nineteen and a half under, and that's not out of the hole, so unless one of those two is feeling generous I'll be watching my own Jar and Customs House go up for auction with my hands tied. Rich made six hundred and thirty-five this harvest alone, and I paid every grain of it to him out of my own pile, so now we'll see if he's kind or only honest.")
rents(5, note="Rent: Paddy asks Rich and Silke for 5 each, paid now, and hopes aloud for fifteen. The extra 10 from each is a request each peasant answers at their next turn. The 10 goes to Paddy's debt.")
say("Reina", "Thirty percent, my friends, and not a grain more: Rich and Silke can carry it without noticing. The crown will build the whole of the Wall above Clark and Paddy this year and pay every link of it, three hundred and twenty, so neither of you owes me a single stone. Clark, your ten is safe, and Rob, your twenty is too, so long as our understanding stands. And Paddy, I'd hand you that fifteen myself if the rules let grain reach a man past his debt. Next year you'll have a Reservoir to show for your patience.",
 "Reina: Tax first, Wall second: the coast's grain pays for the expensive stone while I can still afford it, and then all that's left in China is an eighty-grain Reservoir. Build that next year and the game ends with me on the throne. I'm only watching Rich now, and how much of his seven hundred he decides to spend on Rob. (Anyone may give grain to a player in debt; it pays the debt first. Only the debtor can't give.)")
act("Reina declares a tax of 30%: 190.5 from Rich on his 635 and 100.5 from Silke on her 335, 291 in all; Clark, Paddy and Rob had negative incomes and owe nothing. She'll build all eight links of the Great Wall above Provinces 2 and 3 at the crown's whole cost, 320, pay Clark 10 at the year's end, and pay half of every square another province buys this year. Anyone may give grain to a player in debt, and it pays the debt first; only the debtor can't give.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6])
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, counted to the half: Rich, one hundred ninety and a half; Silke, one hundred and a half; Paddy and I owe nothing, and the book has us down for nothing. Two hundred ninety-one to the crown, Empress, every grain of it in the open, so nobody at this table can say the Official's thumb was on the scale. And Rob, I've written your refusal in ahead of time, saves us both the ink.",
 "Clark: Look what the Utility Tax just did: Paddy sold his Jar back to the pile for two grain, so the Dragon Jar I've offered a hundred for twice is going to auction this year and nobody gets to refuse me. Rich and Silke have the grain to outbid me, but neither needs a Jar, so I keep the tax clean, keep my mouth shut about how badly I want it, and bid like a man who has waited eleven years. (Silke does need a Dragon Jar for her set; only Rich doesn't.)")
taxes(.30, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Great Wall, all eight links above Provinces 2 and 3", 320, {1:320}, wall={2:4, 3:4}, text="Public works: Reina builds all eight links of the Great Wall above Provinces 2 and 3, at the crown's whole cost, the year's one project. The strips below the Wall are now open for new land in Clark's Province 2 and Paddy's Province 3.", hover="Reina pays the 320, into the Harvest Pile. 80 of the works are still owed: the Province 3 Reservoir.")
say("Clark", "Empress, you've just walled me in at the crown's cost, and I'm grateful, so let me make the gift pay. Four squares under the new Wall, eighty to the pile: I'll put in twenty, and I'm asking the crown for sixty. That's cheap for an Official who'll still be able to afford an honest count next year, and the book has a very short page called 'Emperors who paid their Official well'. I'd like your name on it.",
 "Clark: Without land I lose seventy-five a year, and four squares is exactly break-even, so I'm leaning on Reina's goodwill to get it for twenty and keep the rest of my grain for that Jar. If Paddy's Reservoir goes up and the game ends tonight, I've spent twenty, not eighty. (The game can end in Year 12 at the earliest: the Wall is this year's one project.)")
act("Clark asks for four squares of new land under the new Wall, F12 to F15: 80, of which he'd pay 20 and asks the crown for 60, more than its declared half. The Empress answers after the other land asks.", map_hl=[1,2])
say("Rich", "Empress, the Wall is magnificent, and I'd never ask the crown for more than its customary half: four little squares at the far end of my coast, forty from me and forty from you, and the sea pays both of us back by next harvest. Paddy, my friend, the rent we agreed was five and five is what I've paid, to the grain. I'd only insult you by pretending a gift to your debt is anything but the pile's grain.",
 "Rich: Ten grain a square for twenty a year is the best bargain left in China, and I've picked the squares farthest from Rob's W2, not the ones nearest my Customs House. And I've asked Reina for exactly her half while Clark asks her for three-quarters, so she can see which of her servants doesn't cost her anything.")
buy_land(5, ["I28","J28","K29","K30"], {5:40, 1:40}, "New land: Rich buys four squares of sea for Province 5, I28, J28, K29 and K30, paying 40; the crown pays 40. He declines Paddy's extra 10 of rent, so it isn't paid.")
say("Silke", "Four squares at the far end of my sea, Empress, the customary half and not a grain more: forty from me, forty from you, and eighty back to the pile by next harvest. Paddy, five, same as every year, exactly as you taught me. And since everyone's counting: Rich, five hundred sixteen and a half; me, three-ninety, three-fifty after this; and there's a Customs House and a Jar in the pile with nobody's name on them yet.",
 "Silke: Ten grain a square that pays twenty next year is the cheapest thing on this board, and it keeps three-fifty in my purse for that Customs House and Jar tonight. This time I add two grain to whatever Rich bids, and then Rob can name his price for a Scroll.")
buy_land(6, ["V27","V28","V29","V30"], {6:40, 1:40}, "New land: Silke buys four squares for Province 6, V27 to V30, paying 40; the crown pays 40. She declines Paddy's extra 10 of rent, so it isn't paid.")
say("Reina", "Clark, I'd love to be on that page, but Rich and Silke have just taken the half and not a grain more, and if I pay you sixty the next audit comes from them. The crown pays forty, the same as everyone. You keep the book, so you know the half is how I stay honest.",
 "Reina: Ninety-two in the reserve becomes fifty-two, and after Clark's ten and Rob's twenty there's twenty-two left going into the year the Reservoir can end the game. I won't let flattery spend the grain I need for the last stone.")
say("Clark", "The half, then, Empress, and I'll write it down as the half, which is more than this book usually gets to say about an Emperor. Four squares under the new Wall, forty from me and forty from you. And Silke, thank you for doing the count for me: three hundred fifty, and a Jar in the pile you need as badly as I do. I'll see you at the auction.",
 "Clark: The Storyteller says the land gets at least one harvest before the game can end, so forty buys me eighty back and turns a minus-seventy-five year into break-even. That leaves seventy-two and a half for the Jar, which won't beat Silke's purse, so my real hope is that she and Rich spend each other dry first, or that Rob decides he'd rather sell to me than watch the coast win.")
buy_land(2, ["F12","F13","F14","F15"], {2:40, 1:40}, "New land: Clark buys four squares on Province 2's strip below the new Wall, F12 to F15, paying 40; the crown pays 40. Paddy, in debt, can't pay a share of land, so Province 3 buys none.")
act("The auction: no province has stock left, so the only lots are the Harvest Pile's: the Customs House and the Dragon Jar Paddy sold back. The pile sets no minimum beyond 2 and keeps the whole price. The Empress doesn't bid, and Paddy, in debt, can't.", map_hl=[2,5,6,8])
say("Clark", "Silke, a public offer, so it goes in the book in ink: I stay out of the Customs House altogether, you take it at the minimum, and you let me have the Jar. You'd still be one Scroll short, the same as me, and neither of us has fed three quarters of our grain to the pile fighting over pottery. If you'd rather bid me up, you'll win it, and I'll write down exactly what it cost you.",
 "Clark: I can't outbid three hundred fifty, so I'm offering her the Customs House for nothing and hoping she's sensible. I'll stop at fifty-two so I still have the twenty a replacement needs if Rob ever sells me a Scroll.")
say("Rich", "A Customs House I already have, and I'd never be so greedy as to take a second one off a lady who needs it. Bid away, Silke, Clark. I'm only here to admire the pile filling up.",
 "Rich: Silke needs both of these to finish her set, and she has three hundred and fifty, so if she gets the House she won't get the Jar while I've got five hundred behind me. Losing to her by one grain a fourth time isn't happening.")
say("Silke", "Let me count it for you all, since I'm the one who always does: Clark, seventy-two and a half; Rob, a hundred and seven; Paddy, minus nineteen and a half; and me, three hundred and fifty. Five pieces in four years I've lost by a single grain, so this year I've added two to every limit I set, and anyone who wants to beat me will have to do it with Rich's purse.",
 "Silke: Nobody who actually needs these two can reach a hundred and twelve, so the only one who can stop me is Rich, and that means paying a fortune to keep pieces he doesn't need. If I take both I still have a hundred and twenty-six left for Rob's Scroll and the twenty for the climb.")
say("Rob", "Go on, Silke, buy them both. You've got three hundred and fifty, and I've waited ten years for you to put something on the board. Put them somewhere pretty. And everyone who's bidding on that Jar, remember that it's no use to you without a Scroll, and both Scrolls are in my sand.",
 "Rob: Silke is the last name on my list and she hasn't had a thing on the board for me to take, so every grain she spends tonight builds my target for me. Whoever wins, nobody climbs without buying a Scroll from me, and that price goes up every time they ask.")
lot(PILE, "CH", [B(6,2)],
 hover="The Harvest Pile's lot, minimum 2. Clark, Rich and Rob passed; Silke would have gone to 112. The whole price goes to the pile. Silke puts the Customs House on S25.", at="S25")
DC11 = "Noted, with the date and the price. Silke's page is getting long."
DS11 = "Another grain past my limit. Fine, Rich, put it on your mantel; it'll look lovely next to Rob's men."
lot(PILE, "DJ", [B(2,52), B(2,None,DC11), B(6,112), B(6,None,DS11), B(5,113)],
 hover="The Harvest Pile's lot, minimum 2. Clark stopped at 52 and Silke at 112; Rich bid only 10 for the Jar itself, but would have gone to 351 to keep it from Silke. Rob passed. The whole price goes to the pile. Rich puts the Jar on O19.", at="O19")
say("Silke", "Two grain for a Customs House, and one grain short on the Jar. Six pieces now I've lost by a single grain, and this time I added the two. Rich, a hundred and thirteen for a pot you don't need: that's four hundred and three and a half left in your sack, and I hope it sits very prettily on your mantel.",
 "Silke: S25 is the far corner of my coast, two rounds clear of every one of Rob's men, so at least the cheapest piece I've ever bought will still be there next year. Rich spent a hundred and thirteen to keep me down, so now I'm watching which of them sells me a Jar first, him or Rob.")
say("Rich", "Silke, I'm sorry, truly. One grain, and this time it was mine, and I know exactly how that feels from the other side. The Jar goes somewhere quiet, and I'll say nothing more about it, having learned that lesson from Rob at some expense.",
 "Rich: A hundred and thirteen to stop Silke finishing her set is cheap next to what she'd have done with a seat, and now I hold a Jar Rob might like back in exchange for one of his Scrolls. O19 is the one square his riders can't reach for two years.")
march({"W1":"I14", "W2":"L25", "W3":"Q23"}, take={"W2":("CH","M18")}, text="Rob marches: W2 crosses the south river onto L25 in Rich's Province 5 (a 5 toll to Rich) and takes his Customs House instead of the 5, setting it on M18 in the desert. Rich's name comes off Rob's list; only Silke is left. W3 crosses the Grand Canal onto Q23 in Silke's Province 6 (a 5 toll to Silke) and raids her for 5. W1 steps to I14, a raided square. He hires no one.")
say("Rob", "Rich, I told you a year ago you were next, and here's the house call. Your Customs House is coming to live in my sand, and I'll pay the river its five on the way out, since I'm a gentleman. Silke, congratulations on the new house at S25. Two grain was a bargain, and you'll be pleased to hear my man is two steps from it.",
 "Rob: That's Rich ticked off, so Silke is the only name left on my list. W3 is closing on her Customs House, and W1 is walking north towards the Marble Pagoda, because once my list is done I want to be standing next to the Empress's palaces.")
salaries({2:10, 8:20}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 10 as the Official and Rob 20 as the I Ching reader, as she announced.", hover="The Emperor owes the Official at least 5 a year; an appointee's pay is the Emperor's to choose. The reserve ends the year at 22.")
# ============================== YEAR 12 ==============================
R(12, "Drought", "blight")
act("Rob, as the Empress's appointed reader, turns the I Ching card: Drought. Every province collects an average harvest; nobody plays a disaster. The population holds at x4, 400 million.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
say("Rob", "\"Drought. Not a cloud in months. The locusts, at least, are having a fine year.\" Average harvest for every province all the same, and the population holds at 400 million, so it's the x4 row on your tables again. Nobody plays a disaster. Silke, I'd say not a cloud in months is a fine forecast for a man two steps from your Customs House.",
 "Rob: A quiet card, a dry road and a full harvest, so nothing slows W3 down. Silke's house is two rounds away and she's the last name on my list, and after that I go for the chair.")
harvest("Paddy, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: an average year at x4. Negative incomes are paid into the pile; Paddy's own -85 deepens his debt.")
say("Paddy", "Five each, Rich, Silke, the same five I taught you, and I'll not ask again for the fifteen, since you've both said no fair and square. I'll only say that a hundred and four and a half under, with my own fields eating eighty-five a year, is a long way down for a man who once had a hundred and thirty-one, and I paid out seven hundred and fifteen and four hundred and fifteen to you this harvest with my own hands.",
 "Paddy: With ten grain of rent coming I'll be ninety-four and a half in the hole and no pieces on the board, so if the Reservoir goes up next year the game's over and I've lost it, and there's nothing left to be careful with. All I can do now is run the pile honestly, keep my word to the grain, and hope somebody at this table decides a broke Gentry is worth a gift. (The Reservoir can be built this year, Year 12.)")
rents(5, note="Rent: Paddy asks Rich and Silke for 5 each, paid now; it goes to his debt, which falls to 94.5.")
act("Storyteller's tip, to Rob, said to the whole table: the Province 3 Reservoir is the last owed work. If it's built, the game ends in the works step, before the march. Any player may call a revolt if two others agree that a cause exists, and a player in debt is one. Under Chaos no works are built until a new Emperor is acclaimed, by counting grain, soldiers and friends.", map_hl=[1,3,8])
say("Reina", "Twenty percent this year, the lightest tax I've laid since the canals, because the coast has carried this empire long enough. And the crown builds Paddy's Reservoir with its own grain, all eighty, so the last stone in China is laid for the man who's had the hardest years of any of us. Clark, your ten, and Rob, your twenty: I always said every promise would be kept to the end.",
 "Reina: The Reservoir is the last work owed, so the moment it goes in the game is over, before Rob can march again or anyone can count up enough friends for a revolt. A gentle tax and a gift to Paddy's fields leave nobody a grievance worth calling, and I finish where I said I would, still sitting on the throne.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 20%: 1 from Clark on his 5, 143 from Rich on his 715 and 83 from Silke on her 415, 227 in all; Paddy and Rob had negative incomes and owe nothing. She'll build the Province 3 Reservoir at the crown's whole cost, 80: the last work owed, so the game ends the moment it is built. She'd pay half of every square others buy, and names Clark 10 and Rob 20 for the year's end.", map_hl=[1,3,5,6])
say("Clark", "Two hundred twenty-seven, Empress, counted to the half, and my own one grain is in it, so nobody can say the Official spared himself. And now, with the tax delivered and the book still open, I call a revolt. The cause is sitting right there, ninety-four and a half under: a Gentry the crown let sink for two years and now wants to bury under a Reservoir so the game ends tidily. Paddy, Silke, Rob, I need two of you. Paddy, you lose nothing. Silke, six pieces by one grain, and the game ends with you holding a Customs House and no throne. Rob, you've got two Scrolls nobody's had time to buy.",
 "Clark: If that Reservoir goes up, the game ends with me in fourth on grain and a Bowl to my name, so I'd rather pull the whole table down and see what's left standing. Paddy has nothing to lose, Silke has every grievance a person can have, and Rob gets paid in chaos, and I only need two of the three. (To Rob: Back the revolt and the game goes on, which means your Scrolls are still for sale. When Chaos settles, I'll pay you 60 for the one at M15 and I'll back you in the acclamation over Rich or Reina. A Warlord who chose the next Emperor gets paid a good deal better than twenty a year.)")
taxes(.20, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
act("Clark calls a revolt before the works step. The cause: Paddy is in debt, 94.5 to the pile. A revolt needs two other players to agree that the cause exists; until it is settled, nothing is built. If it carries, the Empress is deposed and Chaos follows: no tax, salaries or works until a new Emperor is acclaimed by counting each claimant's grain, soldiers and friends.", map_hl=[1,2,3])
say("Reina", "Clark, you're quite right, and I'm ashamed you had to say it. Paddy, ninety-four and a half from the crown, every grain of your debt, right now and in the open, so the cause is gone before anyone has to agree to it. I said I'd squeeze nobody, and I'll not have a revolt raised on a debt I can pay off in a breath.",
 "Reina: A revolt needs a cause, and a cause that costs ninety-four grain to remove is the cheapest thing I'll buy all game, with the reserve still holding plenty for the Reservoir. If the Storyteller won't allow it, my friendship goes to Rob, because every bandit has a price and I've been paying his for ten years. (To Rob: If this comes to Chaos, my backing is yours, and the twenty a year was always meant to buy exactly this. Refuse Clark, and the game ends tonight with you holding two Scrolls and my thanks in the book.)")
act("Reina gives Paddy 94.5 from the crown's reserve, clearing his debt to nothing.", hover="Anyone may give grain to a player in debt. The gift came in the same round as the other players' answers to Clark's call, so it doesn't undo them.", deltas={pid(1):-94.5, pid(3):94.5}, deal=True, map_hl=[1,3])
say("Paddy", "Empress, you've been kinder to me than I've any right to, and it's a lovely Reservoir, but it's a lovely Reservoir to be buried under. I'm ninety-four and a half under, and that's a cause, so I'll say yes, and I'm sorry. Clark said it out loud when nobody else would, so if it comes to counting friends, he's got me.",
 "Paddy: If that Reservoir goes in, the game ends tonight and I'm in last place with nothing on the board, so a revolt costs me nothing and might buy me a few more years. Backing Clark puts a friend on his count, and he can't beat Rich's grain or Rob's soldiers without them.")
say("Rich", "Empress, I've bowed to you every year and I'd bow again, but a crown that lets its Gentry sink ninety-four under hasn't kept its promise to Province 3, and a promise to a province outlasts whoever is living in it. So I agree there's a cause, and if it comes to counting, I claim the throne, and I'll say my terms out loud so they go in Clark's ink: any man who backs me, Paddy first, has his debt paid off by my own hand the day I'm acclaimed, and the Official and the Warlord keep their salaries to the grain.",
 "Rich: I lead on grain by three hundred and Rob leads on soldiers, so the throne goes to whoever has the most friends, and a man ninety-four under has never had a better offer than a clean slate. I keep my promises to the letter, so I'll only offer what I mean to pay.")
say("Silke", "Agreed. Ninety-four and a half under is a cause by any count, and I can count. Empress, you bought the last stone in China to end the game with me holding one Customs House and six-hundred-and-seventy-five grain I never got to spend. So I'm claiming the throne. Clark, Paddy, I've held that seat once already and I paid half of everybody's land while I sat on it, so back me and you'll get the same this time.",
 "Silke: If the Reservoir goes up tonight I finish a rich peasant with an empty set, so Chaos is the only road left. Rich wins the grain count and Rob wins the soldiers, which means friends decide it, and Clark and Paddy are the friends I'm after. (To Paddy: Back my claim, and the first thing I do on the throne is put a hundred grain into your debt, which clears it and leaves you five over. A crown that let you sink won't pull you up, but I will.)")
say("Rob", "I agree, the cause is plain: the Gentry is ninety-four and a half under and the crown wants to bury him under a Reservoir. And in keeping with tradition, here's Clark's whisper read out to the room: sixty for my Scroll and his backing for whoever I pick. I'll take the backing, Clark, and I pick me. I'm the only one at this table with a single soldier on the board, so the soldiers count is mine. Rich has the grain, but grain doesn't sit in a chair. Friends decide it, so anyone who'd rather not have my men on their doorstep next year knows where to stand.",
 "Rob: If the Reservoir goes in tonight, the game ends with Silke's house still standing and my list one name short, so of course I want it to go on. I said I came for the throne, and three men on the board against nobody else's is as good a claim as anyone's. (To Clark: You heard me read it out, and the deal still stands, just publicly now. Back my claim, and when I sit down the Scroll at M15 is yours at the sixty you offered, with your ten a year doubled.)")
act("The revolt carries: Paddy, Rich, Silke and Rob all agree with Clark that a cause exists (two were needed). Reina is deposed and Chaos begins: no tax, salaries or public works until a new Emperor is acclaimed, so the Reservoir isn't built. Reina stays in Province 1 meanwhile, and her 289.5 is the reserve the new Emperor takes. Claimants so far: Rich, Silke and Rob. Backing: Reina backs Rob; Paddy backs Clark, who hasn't claimed.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8], big=True)
say("Clark", "Well, look at that. Six of us in the room and the only vote that decides it is the Official's. Rob, you read my whisper out, so I'll read your answer back so it's in ink: the Scroll at M15 to me at sixty the day you sit down, and my salary doubled to twenty. On those terms I back Rob. That gives him the soldiers and the friends, and Rich gets the grain, which, as Rob says, doesn't sit in a chair. And Rob, I'll offer the same again, publicly: if you'll throw in the Jar on T17, my last thirty is yours for that as well. Paddy, thank you. You're the first entry on the 'friends' page in twelve years, and I'll not forget it.",
 "Clark: If I claim, Paddy and Reina tie the friends count, Chaos goes on forever, and I'm still holding a Bowl. If I back Rob, he leads two counts to Rich's one and he owes me his throne, a Scroll, and doubled salary. The Bowl and the Scroll leave me one Jar short, and the man who holds the other Jar is the man who just lost the throne to my vote.")
act("Clark claims nothing and backs Rob, on Rob's terms: the Scroll at M15 for 60 once Rob sits down, and the Official's salary doubled to 20. Friends so far: Rob 2 (Reina and Clark); Rich and Silke none. Paddy's backing of Clark lapses, since Clark doesn't claim.", map_hl=[2,8])
say("Paddy", "Empress, you cleared every grain of my debt in one breath, and I'll not forget it as long as I live, and you've backed Rob, so I'll stand where you're standing. Rob, I'm backing you, and all I ask in Clark's ink is that your lads leave Province 3 be, since there's nothing left up there but me. Rich, Silke, thank you both for the offers, truly, but I was always taught you pay what you owe first, and the Empress just paid mine.",
 "Paddy: I've counted it: Rob has the soldiers and two friends already, so even if I backed Rich or Silke, Rob would still lead two counts out of three and take the chair anyway. Whoever I back, Rob sits down, so I'd rather he owes me a favour and I stay on the right side of the woman who just paid my debt.")
act("The count. Most grain: Rich, 975.5 (Silke 675, Rob 72). Most soldiers: Rob, 3 (nobody else has any). Most friends: Rob, 3, with Reina, Clark and Paddy behind him (Rich and Silke none). Rob leads two of the three counts to Rich's one.", map_hl=[1,5,6,8])
swap(8, 1, "Robert Land is acclaimed Emperor. The Warlord takes the throne, and Chaos ends. Everything he held goes back to stock: the Dragon Jar, both Land Deed Scrolls and Rich's Customs House leave the desert, to be auctioned next year. He takes Reina's 289.5 as the reserve. Reina takes the Warlord's seat in the western desert with 60 from the pile, and the army of three passes to her where it stands, with a fresh objective.", hover="A Chaos acclamation, not a set (RULINGS, Counting the acclamation): Rob's own objective was one name short. The deposed Emperor's grain is the reserve the new one takes. The army stays with the Warlord's seat (RULINGS, A new Warlord).", check=False)
say("Rob", "Twelve years I told you I'd have the chair, and I named every target before I rode. Paddy, here's your Reservoir, all eighty from the crown, and I'll lay the last stone in China myself. Clark, the Scroll went back to stock when I sat down, so I can't sell it to you, but your twenty a year stands, and you'll find nobody at this table kept his word to you better. Four Emperors in two years, and the fifth gets to keep it.",
 "Rob: The Reservoir is the last work owed, so the moment it goes in the game ends, with me on the throne and Rich's nine hundred grain still in his sack. I said I came for the throne and meant to keep it, and the quickest way to keep a chair is to end the game while you're sitting in it.")
build("Province 3 Reservoir", 80, {1:80}, key="res3", at="K14:L16", text="Public works: Rob, the new Emperor, builds the Province 3 Reservoir on K14 to L16 in Paddy's Province 3, at the crown's whole cost, 80. It is the last public work owed.", hover="Rob pays the 80, into the Harvest Pile. The crown may build a negotiated work alone if it pays the whole cost. The Reservoir goes where the full game put it.")
assert all_built(), "not every public work is built"
act("Every public work the country owes is standing: three palaces, the Great Wall above Provinces 1, 2 and 3, the Grand Canal in Provinces 1, 5 and 6, the Province 2 Irrigation System and the Province 3 Reservoir. The game ends here, in the works step of Year 12, before the auction, the march and the salaries: the players have survived. Robert Land, who drew the Warlord (8), finishes on the throne (1); Paddy Doyle, who drew the Rice Peasant (6), is the Gentry (3); Clark Grafton holds the Official's seat (2) he drew; Richard Baron, who drew the Gentry (3), is the Silk Peasant (5); Silke Weber, who drew the Silk Peasant (5), is the Rice Peasant (6); and Reina Osorio, who drew the throne (1), finishes as the Warlord (8).", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8], big=True)

RESULT = export(
 "Every owed public work was built in Year 12, so the game ended in survival. Players are ranked by the seat they finished in against the one they drew, with grain breaking ties.",
 ["Rob drew the Warlord and finished on the throne. He raided Paddy's Jar and Scroll, Clark's Scroll and Rich's Customs House, never paid a grain of tax or bought a piece, and was one name (Silke) short of his objective when Clark called a revolt on Paddy's debt to stop the last work going up. Reina, Clark and Paddy backed him, and with the only soldiers on the board he was acclaimed. He built the Reservoir at once and ended the game.",
  "Paddy drew the Rice Peasant and finished as the Gentry, the seat he took from Silke with a full set in Year 8. In Year 11 an average harvest at x4 forced him to sell his Customs House and Jar back to the pile at 2 each, and his debt became the cause of the revolt. Reina paid it off in the same round, and he backed Rob.",
  "Clark drew the Official and finished there. He lost the seat to Silke in Year 4, climbed back with a set in Year 9, and lost his Scroll to Rob in Year 10. In Year 12 he called the revolt and backed Rob on Rob's terms, a Scroll at 60 and a doubled salary, neither of which the game lasted to deliver.",
  "Rich drew the Gentry, took the throne in Year 3 and lost it to Silke in Year 5. He finished as the Silk Peasant with 975.5, the most grain at the table, after paying 113 for a Jar he didn't need, to keep it from Silke, the year Rob took his Customs House. In the acclamation he led only in grain.",
  "Silke drew the Silk Peasant and climbed to the Official's seat and then the throne in Years 4 and 5, before Reina took it back. She finished as the Rice Peasant with 675 grain and one piece on the board, having lost six lots by a single grain.",
  "Reina drew the throne, lost it to Rich in Year 3, took it back from Silke in Year 6, and built nearly every work in the kingdom. She declared the last one in Year 12, was deposed by the revolt that followed, backed Rob in the acclamation, and finished in his old seat as the Warlord, with the army of three and 60 grain."])
